id:6410847e5d7fc423fdc8f587 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """We are St Luke's Hospice based in Harrow, Middlesex and we care for patients whose illness are no longer curable.  We are heavily reliant on our wonderful volunteers  to help raise vital funds for the hospice. 

Every penny of profit from our shops goes into providing specialist care to people approaching the end of their life in Harrow & Brent. 

Volunteering for St Luke's Hospice can give you the opportunity to gain experience and develop skills in stock processing and logistics with training and ongoing support provided.

The chance to work with a friendly team and  flexibility to fit with your lifestyle.

Our volunteers have the sense of fulfilment from raising money for St Luke’s Hospice and anyone wishing to join our team would be more than welcome. 



""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/b1207222-2283-434a-9409-438ad42d4a06_2023-03-14T14:28:13.649Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "St Luke's Hospice";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.stlukes-hospice.org/>.
id:64108511a7ae583a6bcaf4d3 :activityDescription """Shop Volunteer

Whether you want to gain retail experience, have some spare time, or want to give back to your community, St Luke’s Hospice Shops are a great place to enjoy the social aspect of volunteering and can give you the chance to gain work experience, learn new skills or put your existing talents to use. 

The success of our shops is largely down to the fantastic commitment and enthusiasm of our Volunteers. Either on the shop floor serving customers, or out the back sorting and processing stock ready for sale, we couldn’t do what we do without the time and effort of our incredible Volunteers.

Previous experience is not essential - we have a dedicated team and set-up to help you with this exciting opportunity – all you need is enthusiasm, a confident manner and an interest in retail – we’ll give you a full induction, as well as ongoing training and support.

Welcome to St Luke’s

St Luke’s Hospice Shops raise vital funds to help provide free specialist care for people approaching the end of their life in Harrow and Brent. 

Our friendly and welcoming team of staff and volunteers at our 18 shops love meeting new people from all walks of life. We're always looking for new volunteers to join us and make the service we offer as special as possible for the people of Harrow and Brent. 

What we need your help with

•	Serving customer on the till and shop floor and providing good all-round customer service
•	Keeping the shop floor clean, tidy and well stocked
•	Helping with window and shop floor displays
•	Sorting and processing donations ready for the shop floor 
•	Promoting St Luke’s Hospice campaigns and in-store sales to customers

What we can offer

•	The opportunity to gain experience and develop skills in retail and customer service with training and ongoing support provided
•	The chance to work with a friend team
•	Flexibility to fit with your lifestyle 
•	The sense of fulfilment from raising money for St Luke’s Hospice
•	Reimbursement of reasonable travel expenses (capped)

If you want to join the team, or would like more information or a chat about the role, apply today and one of the Team with contact you directly to discuss.

""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6410847e5d7fc423fdc8f587;
                            :activityTitle "Retail Volunteer".
id:6410854b2063ec5bf05bf472 :activityDescription """Shop Volunteer

Whether you want to gain retail experience, have some spare time, or want to give back to your community, St Luke’s Hospice Shops are a great place to enjoy the social aspect of volunteering and can give you the chance to gain work experience, learn new skills or put your existing talents to use. 

The success of our shops is largely down to the fantastic commitment and enthusiasm of our Volunteers. Either on the shop floor serving customers, or out the back sorting and processing stock ready for sale, we couldn’t do what we do without the time and effort of our incredible Volunteers.

Previous experience is not essential - we have a dedicated team and set-up to help you with this exciting opportunity – all you need is enthusiasm, a confident manner and an interest in retail – we’ll give you a full induction, as well as ongoing training and support.

Welcome to St Luke’s

St Luke’s Hospice Shops raise vital funds to help provide free specialist care for people approaching the end of their life in Harrow and Brent. 

Our friendly and welcoming team of staff and volunteers at our 18 shops love meeting new people from all walks of life. We're always looking for new volunteers to join us and make the service we offer as special as possible for the people of Harrow and Brent. 

What we need your help with

•	Serving customer on the till and shop floor and providing good all-round customer service
•	Keeping the shop floor clean, tidy and well stocked
•	Helping with window and shop floor displays
•	Sorting and processing donations ready for the shop floor 
•	Promoting St Luke’s Hospice campaigns and in-store sales to customers

What we can offer

•	The opportunity to gain experience and develop skills in retail and customer service with training and ongoing support provided
•	The chance to work with a friend team
•	Flexibility to fit with your lifestyle 
•	The sense of fulfilment from raising money for St Luke’s Hospice
•	Reimbursement of reasonable travel expenses (capped)

If you want to join the team, or would like more information or a chat about the role, apply today and one of the Team with contact you directly to discuss.

""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6410847e5d7fc423fdc8f587;
                            :activityTitle "Warehouse Volunteer".
id:641085b52063ec5bf05bf49e :activityDescription """Furniture Shop Volunteer

If you have some spare time and want to give back to your community St Luke’s Hospice Furniture shop based on Kenton Road, Harrow, is great place to volunteer.  Joining our friendly team can also give you the chance to gain work experience, learn new skills or put your existing skills to good use. 

At the furniture shop we are looking for volunteers who are physically fit and able to lift and move furniture as well as assembling it.  Every volunteer will receive Manual Handling Training and are instructed not to attempt to lift heavy items without assistance.  
Skate trolleys and sack barrows will be available at the back of the shop, with bags and boxes available to all volunteers to enable them to repack donated goods before placing them on to the collections bay. Guidelines on stacking bags and boxes is included in our health and safety manual book. 
What we need your help with

•	Serving customers on the till and shop floor providing good all-round customer service
•	Volunteers need to be physically fit and able to lift, move and assemble furniture 
•	Helping with window and shop floor displays
•	Sorting and processing donations ready for the shop floor 
•	Promoting St Luke’s Hospice campaigns and in-store sales to customers

What we can offer

•	The opportunity to gain experience and develop skills in retail and customer service with training and ongoing support provided
•	The chance to work with a friendly team
•	Manual handling training provided
•	The sense of fulfilment from raising money for St Luke’s Hospice
•	Reimbursement of reasonable travel expenses (capped)




""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6410847e5d7fc423fdc8f587;
                            :activityTitle "Furniture shop Volunteer".
id:641096af1097b43af15de362 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """A parent never imagines their child will be diagnosed with a life limiting or life threatening condition. But when the unimaginable happens, Acorns steps in, helping families cope at every stage of their child’s life and beyond, wherever and whenever they need it. The need for children’s hospice care never stops which is why we are here to provide specialist palliative care 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.

At Acorns our dedicated team offers psychological, emotional and practical support. Our team of health and social care professionals provide a range of services, including bereavement support and advocacy, as well as a number of dedicated support groups, helping to reduce the isolation some families can face.""";
                            :organisationName "Acorns Children's Hospice";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.acorns.org.uk/>.
id:64109be61097b43af15de474 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """St Leonard’s Hospice is an independent charity, providing specialist palliative care, and support for local people with life-limiting illnesses. 

We’re dedicated to providing support tailored to the specific needs of patients, and their families and carers, with a wide range of services offered in a relaxed and friendly environment where care is taken personally.

We couldn’t carry out the important work we do without a committed and talented team of volunteers – people of all ages, and from all walks of life who help us care, support and raise funds.

Volunteers are a much valued part of our Hospice team, providing support across a wide variety of departments. Some help support patients and families, including bereavement support, some help in administrative roles while others keep the Hospice gardens looking wonderful!
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/c293b970-4f4d-40f5-9eef-235803aa72af_2023-05-04T17:45:09.439Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "St Leonard's Hospice";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://stleonardshospice.org.uk/how-can-you-help-us/volunteering-at-st-leonards-york/>.
id:6410baa1bc31aec18e3dab80 :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50899,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription """We look after people over 18 from across the Sheffield region, with all kinds of terminal illnesses, including end stage neurological, heart, kidney and lung conditions, cancer and HIV. Last year, we helped around 1,800 patients – some of who came to the hospice but many more were looked after at home by our team of community nurses, who help treat and alleviate pain, support and counsel and in some cases, help patients to die at home, if that’s what they want. Most people want to die at home, and we help to enable that to happen if we can. It’s not just patients that we help either – we extend support, counselling and practical help to family members too.

Everything we do is dependent on over 250 amazing members of staff and a phenomenal team of over 700 volunteers, all of them giving time, expertise and effort. Besides that, we have a legion of supporters and donors who raise money tirelessly to make it all possible. In fact, of the £11.5 million it takes to deliver our services this year, we need to raise £7.5 million of that from donations… and that means a lot of running, cake-baking and pie-eating help from our friends. Every customer at our charity shops helps too, adding to the income we receive from our retail outlets across the city.

Last year, our community team made 6,289 visits to patients in their own homes across Sheffield, helping to support them and the other healthcare professionals caring for them. Meanwhile our In Patient Centre provided 24-hour specialist palliative care for for over 300 people. And every year individual programmes of therapies, treatments and advice through our Patient and Family Support services help over 200 patients to live independently at home for as long as possible.
Each year, our work touches the lives of around 6,000 people.
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/37a93bfc-0171-4d6a-a985-2d5487947dce_2023-03-14T18:19:11.955Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "St Luke's Hospice ";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://stlukeshospice.org.uk/>.
id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription """Working locally in the UK and around the world, we take a bird’s eye view of all the issues that the natural world can face. We look at the bigger picture, and focus in on the detail too. 

We’re in a nature and climate emergency, and individual species, habitats and landscapes are in jeopardy.

But we focus on hope. By working together with a renewed strength and purpose, we will provide solutions to ensure the natural world recovers, thrives and flourishes.

Our belief
We’re all connected by the wonder of nature. The health of the natural world is fundamental to the survival of all species and has a right to flourish. Nature is in crisis, by working together we can save it.

Our purpose
To advance the conservation of birds, wildlife and the natural world, by protecting and restoring habitats and landscapes, saving species, sharing knowledge and connecting people to nature.

The world we want to see
A shared world where wildlife, wild places and all people thrive. 

We’re all connected by the wonder of nature. The health of the natural world is fundamental to the survival of all species and has a right to flourish. Nature is in crisis, by working together we can save it.
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/570e04a1-d41b-4e47-82d5-14459581014c_2023-03-15T10:20:33.234Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "RSPB";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.rspb.org.uk/helping-nature/support-the-rspb/volunteering>.
id:6411a1316cda783f8fe77887 :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """When there is no cure, we provide the care you need.

Havens Hospices provides palliative nursing and supportive care to adults and children living with complex or incurable conditions.

Our specialist Care Teams can support them and their family living throughout illness, death and bereavement in the comfort of their own home and through our hospice services, Fair Havens in Southend-on-Sea and Little Havens in Benfleet.

We focus on their quality of life, caring for individual physical, emotional, psychological and spiritual needs. We don’t charge a penny for any of our care services. Our hospice care can only exist because of the support and generosity of the community.

Together, we’re ‘Making every day count.’""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/05295a27-6706-4d7d-99e7-583a71b78575_2023-03-16T10:20:09.093Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Havens Hospices";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.havenshospices.org.uk/about-us/>.
id:6411bca29fcc46408f5a4e13 :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895;
                            :organisationDescription "Re-engage is a charity that is positive about older age and committed to fighting loneliness so that people can have social lives and friendship groups however old they are. We inspire and enable meaningful connections and shared experiences within communities across the UK for people over 75 facing loneliness and social isolation. Our volunteers work together to create better communities and help to enrich the lives of our members by giving them something to look forward to.     ";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/887e676a-01e7-49a8-84a4-ffbbb0237e55_2023-03-15T12:40:02.333Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Re-engage";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.reengage.org.uk/>.
id:6411c79efc94a5f7fcfde148 :activityDescription """Impact volunteers help us to evaluate our work so that we can understand the impact of our services. This can involve interviewing older people or asking them to complete questionnaires.

The role asks you to give around two hours each week (or eight to 10 hours per month). You will spend most of your time surveying older people on the phone and gathering their responses, and there may be interviewing opportunities for those volunteers who would like to do so. This is a flexible and remote voluntary role that can be carried out from home.

You will need one reference and to complete an ID check for this role.

To apply:  https://www.reengage.org.uk/volunteer/new-volunteers/apply/

Tasks could include:
Conducting evaluation surveys with older people over the phone.
Collecting evaluation responses on an online form during calls and submitting to Re-engage.
Compiling and inputting data from written surveys into spreadsheets or systems.
Conducting evaluation interviews with older people over the phone (this requires prior experience and / or qualifications).
Following all guidance in your impact volunteer training modules to ensure that you handle all personal data securely, are aware of our safeguarding framework, and that the activities are completed in line with our charitable aims.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6411bca29fcc46408f5a4e13;
                            :activityTitle "Re-engage - Impact volunteer".
id:6411d4f2c9f517b85449fd41 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba5089b;
                            :organisationDescription """Since 1891, we’ve been working for a better future for dogs and the people who love them.  

When a dog is in distress, we care for them. When a dog needs a home, we find them a loving family. When an owner needs a helping hand (or paw) – or they just can’t cope, we’re ready to step in. 

For every dog, for every owner, we’re by their side through thick and thin, throughout their lives. The bond they have is special to us. It changes lives, making each day happier and more complete. It’s why we believe A dog is for life.® """;
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/a8e4e72b-032b-42ad-9dbd-edee8803f8cb_2023-03-15T14:23:46.393Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Dogs Trust";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.dogstrust.org.uk/>.
id:6411fb368eab8ac79a6603e4 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription "RNIB, the Royal National Institute of Blind People, is the UK's leading sight loss charity. We offer practical and emotional support to blind and partially sighted people, their families and carers. We raise awareness of the experiences of blind and partially sighted people and campaign for change to make our society more accessible for all. We want to change our world so there are no barriers to people with sight loss.";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/f0b2a8a7-f4d6-426b-8497-541a4f8d08d3_2023-03-15T17:07:02.195Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "RNIB";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.rnib.org.uk/>.
id:641202b737fe2c340585f4d1 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription "Informal group caring for local greenspace, community gardening";
                            :organisationName "Friends of Mile End Meadow";
                            :organisationWebsite <http://stockportgreenspace.org.uk/Members/Friends_of_Mile_End_Meadow.htm>.
id:6412288a05e7ccb6304832ad :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """Through fun, friendship, challenge and adventure we empower girls to find their voice, inspiring them to discover the best in themselves and to make a positive difference in their community.  

More than 300,000 girls meet as Rainbows, Brownies, Guides and Rangers. We fire their imaginations through challenges and adventures for all personalities and abilities, from 4 to 18 years old.

Guiding is so much more than an after school club.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/55599d81-99e6-448f-a91b-653f6d618097_2023-03-25T17:09:11.810Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Girlguiding Weybridge district";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.girlguiding.org.uk/>.
id:6412e408c581b3b6aa6e698d :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """Are you looking for a fulfilling opportunity to volunteer in East or West Kent local or nearby to where you live, directly supporting those who may be lonely or struggling to cope with daily tasks while navigating Cancer?

With over 10 years' experience in supporting connections between volunteers and those navigating Cancer (and over 40 years' experience as a charity), the Crossroads Macmillan Volunteer Service can offer flexible, rewarding volunteering avenues for you to explore; get in touch today and make an immediate, positive impact on someone's life - a little bit of time can truly make a huge difference.
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/23763aeb-e650-417b-88a1-7d05d860ae8d_2023-03-16T09:40:24.402Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Crossroads Macmillan Volunteer Service ";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.crossroadskent.org/volunteering>.
id:64132998dc0c2d1eec171145 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription "Active Prospects is a charitable organisation with over 30 years’ experience providing a range of specialised accommodation and support services across Surrey and Sussex. Each year we support around 250 people with learning disabilities, autism, mental health needs, acquired brain injury and those with complex needs to live full and aspiring lives.";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/ac7a8775-a9e7-468f-98bc-98d62021df47_2023-03-16T14:37:11.797Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Active Prospects ";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.activeprospects.org.uk/>.
id:64132e2747ee134e99d35360 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50899;
                            :organisationDescription "As the founding branch of the organisation, Central London Samaritans has been providing emotional support to people who are struggling to cope for almost 70 years. Our volunteers are here to listen around the clock, every single day of the year.";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/0c0c4c03-5ddc-41ad-bb21-7d08f8f22060_2023-03-16T14:56:39.556Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Central London Samaritans";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.samaritans.org/branches/central-london/>.
id:64133c00f55a359b1f6936a3 :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895;
                            :organisationDescription """Good Neighbours is simple: we connect over 50s to befrienders, building positive relationships across Coventry.

In 2021 alone, we connected 609 over 50s to befrienders.

One of our society’s biggest problems is loneliness, particularly in older people. It affects mental and physical health, but can be tackled so easily! Good Neighbours was developed to tackle loneliness in older generations. It connects volunteer befrienders with a spare half hour to telephone or visit an older person.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/fcd5dc51-20ab-4ced-9bd0-e9042b20252d_2023-03-16T15:55:44.237Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Good Neighbours Coventry";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://hopecoventry.org.uk/good-neighbours/>.
id:64133f2ac9d1c901888734cf :activityDescription """Active Prospects is a charitable organisation with nearly 30 years’ experience providing a range of specialised accommodation and support services across Surrey and Sussex. We support over 300 people with learning disabilities, autism, mental health needs, acquired brain injury and those with complex needs to live full and aspirational lives.

What you’ll be doing:

Meet and greet anyone arriving at the head office in Reigate, signing in visitors, staff and people we support.
Answering incoming calls and redirecting them to other office staff.
Dealing with initial calls and message taking where necessary.
Opening the back and front door when intercoms are buzzed.
Date stamping and distributing daily post.
Franking outgoing post.
Booking meeting rooms electronically.
Taking mail to the Post Office at 4pm each day.
Laminating signs or pages.

What skills do I need?
Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to remain calm if an unexpected situation occurs.
Confident with answering and transferring telephone calls.
Confident at using Outlook (or similar), Word (or similar).
Respectful to all visitors and patient with staff and PWS.
Previous experience in an office/reception environment highly desirable.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64132998dc0c2d1eec171145;
                            :activityTitle "Reception/Admin Volunteer".
id:64135259bab6b2821fb75dee :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """Everything we do protects children today and prevents abuse tomorrow, to transform society for every childhood. 

Together, we can stop child abuse and neglect. Through the collective power of our staff, volunteers, supporters and partners, and our 100 years of experience; we will move closer to achieving that goal.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/4535af76-6c7d-4335-a8f8-05e05422a06c_2023-03-16T17:31:04.614Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "NSPCC";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.nspcc.org.uk/>.
id:64143b4cf273f31d0cca6215 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897;
                            :organisationDescription """Volunteer Centre Dorset enables, supports and champions volunteers and volunteering across Dorset. 

We recruit and match volunteers with opportunities in organisations who need volunteers. We support both volunteers and organisations with training, guidance and practical things like DBS checks and ID badges. 

We are a strategic partner for the local authority and NHS in delivering commissioned projects that support our community. """;
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/a148f58a-99b9-4fe6-81b1-0d3bea90de15_2023-03-17T10:05:00.245Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Volunteer Centre Dorset";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.volunteeringdorset.org.uk/>.
id:64143c4cf273f31d0cca6248 :activityDescription """Community volunteers will be given the option to take on a task to help someone who lives local to them. This will be to do a task that you might normally ask a friend or neighbour to do. Task might include: shopping, walking a dog, preparing a ready meal, collecting a prescription, mowing a lawn, helping pack/unpack or declutter, moving some furniture, or just popping in to check someone is doing ok.  

We need help from Community Volunteers every day, so please do sign up to see if there are small tasks that need to be done in your area. """;
                            :activityOrganisation id:64143b4cf273f31d0cca6215;
                            :activityTitle "Community Volunteer".
id:641467dde7584a6b4d61e011 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """We bring light to families affected by childhood cancer by providing emotional, practical and financial support. We bring hope by investing in vital research to improve the outcomes and lives of children with cancer.

Candlelighters are experts at providing support to families in Yorkshire affected by childhood cancer. We are there from diagnosis and for as long as is needed, wherever that help is needed, whether on the ward, at home or in the community. We understand the complexities and impacts of a childhood cancer diagnosis, and we work hard to help ordinary families through extraordinary circumstances.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/9b06b695-aaf5-4015-abd5-21593a3e2d86_2023-03-17T13:15:09.088Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Candlelighters";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.candlelighters.org.uk/>.
id:641475b60ed216fc40d98030 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50899,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription "In partnership with the Kent Resilience Forum, the Environment Agency provide training for volunteer community Flood Wardens. Flood Wardens are volunteers trained to help and prepare people in local communities that are at risk of flooding. They play an important role in the preparing a community flood plan and putting it in to practice.";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/b076c59e-52a0-41a3-9117-7223e52223fc_2023-03-17T14:14:13.643Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Environment Agency";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.kentprepared.org.uk/flooding>.
id:6414768d0ed216fc40d980b6 :activityDescription """Help Your Community - Become a volunteer Flood Warden

Flood wardens are volunteers trained to help and prepare people in local communities that are at risk of flooding. They play an important role in the preparing a community flood plan and putting it in to practice.

They also provide a vital link between a local community and those responsible for responding to a flood.

Helping the community before, during and after a flood
​
…before
Identify problem areas within your community
Identify vulnerable people and properties
Write a community flood plan
Monitor watercourses within the community
Encourage sign up to free Flood Warnings
                                   
…during
Communicate the risk of flooding within the community
Help co-ordinate the community flood plan
Heed emergency services advice at all times
Share information from the Environment Agency and emergency services with residents
Encourage residents to take action
Collect flood event information

…after
Assist in the post-flood clear up if required
Continue to act as a communication channel
Collect post-flood data and photos
Assist in the review of the community flood plan

You will be the eyes and ears of the community and will help develop your local flood plan.

You can choose to meet with other flood wardens in your community as often as you like, typically twice a year.

These meetings could be used for:
Updating your flood plan
Checking contact details
Talking about ways to recruit new wardens
Maintaining interest for existing wardens""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:641475b60ed216fc40d98030;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteer Community Flood Warden".
id:64147964bdd313f63d021734 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897;
                            :organisationDescription """Volunteering Matters is a national charity that brings people and organisations together through the power of volunteering to overcome some of society’s most complex issues. We have a unique approach to social action that takes the time to understand people’s needs, hopes and aspirations, so that everyone can create positive change. Our belief is that communities have the power, knowledge and energy to improve themselves from within and that we are most effective when we work with people, not on their behalf.
We believe that everyone in the UK should have the opportunity to thrive.  We partner with communities to overcome adversity, tackling social isolation and loneliness, improving health, developing skills and opportunity and making sure young people can lead change.
And we turn local knowledge and energy into action and progress, building stronger communities and a better future for all. 
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/73497d4e-3f9d-4d60-8945-275cf99fe049_2023-04-17T15:55:07.075Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Volunteering Matters";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://volunteeringmatters.org.uk/>.
id:6414906b4d58b0689735761c :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50899;
                            :organisationDescription """Happy Baby Community (HBC) aims to build a safe, resilient and thriving community for pregnant women and women with young children who have fled war, violence and traffickers and are seeking international protection in the UK. 

We run four outreach groups for mothers and babies across London where mums and children can access hot meals, clothing donations, perinatal and welfare advice, children's activities, and more. 
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/96e3aa06-b0fd-408c-876b-55356d0f254a_2023-03-17T16:08:11.242Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Happy Baby Community";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.happybabycommunity.org.uk/>.
id:641490b3cd1e6f3754bafad8 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription "";
                            :organisationName "Greenwich,Deptford and Rotherhithe Sea Cadets";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.sea-cadets.org/greenwich>.
id:641492964d58b0689735787c :activityDescription """At Happy Baby Community, we provide safe and resilient communities for pregnant women and women with young children who have fled war, violence and traffickers and are seeking asylum in the UK. We want to make sure we protect and empower each other, through shared knowledge and understanding. We meet to learn from one another and to feel strong and supported - able to work, study, volunteer, and help others in need. 

We are looking for female childcare volunteers to join our vibrant community!


This particular role is to help run the children’s program. It is desired, although not essential, for volunteers to have experience in childcare, providing activities and sessions for children ages 0-5. The children’s activities could be storytelling, song or dance time, crafts, or just general playtime with the children. Volunteers will also need to set up and tidy away after each session.
We are looking for volunteers interested in making a regular weekly commitment of around 3 hours from 10am-1pm every Monday.

This group serves as a safe space for mothers with babies and pregnant mums to access our services, which include perinatal support, children's activities (singing and storytelling), yoga and dance classes, practical support, and more. """;
                            :activityOrganisation id:6414906b4d58b0689735761c;
                            :activityTitle "Children's Program Support (Acton)".
id:6414c018ba2db48cb2ad2068 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription "Our emotional support volunteers offer confidential emotional support to female survivors of sexual violence and abuse by providing our helpline service, email support service and live chat service.";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/84f6d49a-d00d-489b-b716-7dd49511b32a_2023-03-17T19:31:36.096Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Cambridge Rape Crisis Centre ";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://cambridgerapecrisis.org.uk/emotional-support-volunteers/>.
id:64171c1ec0ab262e3c06125d :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription """We hold regular Repair Cafes (education/fixing/skill swapping/upcycling events) in Haringey. Our Volunteers offer local residents advice/help on how best to mend cherished broken/faulty household items.
We enable local people to join the community repair movement by getting together in a Social Cinema, by sharing their expertise, and learning.
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/2e168844-f68d-4d6a-9740-ddd7e9890aee_2023-03-19T14:28:45.886Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Haringey Fixers";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://haringeyfixers.org/>.
id:64171e56c0ab262e3c06135d :activityDescription """We hold Repair Cafés on the 1st (Lordship Rec N17 6NU) and 3rd (Broadwater Farm N17 6HE) Saturdays of every month - details: https://HaringeyFixers.org
What do you do with a chair when the leg as come loose? A lamp that no longer works? A toaster that doesn't toast? A holey jumper? Throw it away? No way! We are hosting afternoons of fixing and skill-sharing.

Repair Cafés are  a community even where local people get together to fix broken things and learn new skills. Repair Cafés aim to help people learn to fix and\\or help others to fix, to bring about a shift in consumerism and to throw less away. We skill-share and build community. We try to match people who want to fix something broken with experienced fixers who can help. Don't leave it in a cupboard or throw it away - fix it. We help you get into the habit of repairing and reusing things, saving you money and helping us all.

Let us know on the order form what time suits you and what you are bringing.

If you have skills you can share, please bring along any tools you use (we already have a wide range) and help us help others - let us know on the order form you can help. Swap skills too - you help to fix mine and I'll help to fix your broken stuff.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64171c1ec0ab262e3c06125d;
                            :activityTitle "Repair Café".
id:64176303612dbc7168ef08d6 :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription """We are the local hospice of choice for all those who need us and an example of excellence in end of life care.

At Trinity we believe that every moment matters. We go above and beyond to help our patients and their families make the most of every moment they have left.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/2f44c679-690e-47c5-8099-478435fcea0a_2023-03-19T19:31:14.890Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Royal Trinity Hospice ";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.royaltrinityhospice.london/>.
id:64179b76c83113223bf1343a :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50899,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription "The SVP works to empower people who are struggling financially, out of employment, education, social networks, and community support. We want everyone regardless of who they are to feel that they belong in their community and have the opportunity to thrive. Through our social justice work, befriending and our community support projects which include our St Vincent's community shops, furniture stores, support centres, supported accommodation and our social enterprise. We provide a wide range of free services and practical support tailored to the needs of the individual helping them to build brighter futures. ";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/ccea4f9b-f0d4-4fa4-8351-1cd500ba1017_2023-03-22T12:56:47.481Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "St Vincent de Paul Society (England and Wales) ";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.svp.org.uk/>.
id:641817f0788518b891da582d :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """There are currently around 270 ARCh volunteers working with more than 100 primary schools in Oxfordshire but we are facing huge demand for more as schools value our support so highly. Year on year, schools report dramatic improvements in children's reading ability, communication skills and confidence and self esteem after a year of support.
After successfully completing our application, interview and checking process, volunteers attend two initial training sessions and make a commitment to volunteer for at least a year.
ARCh volunteers come from all sorts of backgrounds. No experience is necessary.
All they need is to:
have good communication skills
like children
enjoy reading
be patient and adaptable.

Initial training covers topics including:

how children learn to read
the problems they can face
examples of books and games to use
safeguarding

We provide books and games, with the opportunity to exchange them regularly. 
Each volunteer is assigned 3 children to work with and they enjoy 2 \"happy half hours\" with them each week - term time only.
Resources are personalised to suit each child's ability and interests.

We pay travelling expenses between home and school.

We introduce volunteers into their school and provide ongoing support in the form of school visits, ongoing training and meetings each term.

Volunteers enjoy their ARCh time as much as the children! 

Please join us to help more Oxfordshire children to catch up with vital reading and communication skills, enhancing their well-being and improving overall life chances.
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/1f908fc9-7d01-4155-88b2-6867356397e9_2023-03-20T08:23:11.853Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Assisted Reading for Children (ARCh)";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.archoxfordshire.org.uk/>.
id:6418219f454a130154f4171a :activityDescription """Volunteers share  2 \"happy half hours\" with each child each week, sharing books, games and conversation""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:641817f0788518b891da582d;
                            :activityTitle "Inspiring children to enjoy reading".
id:64182575c656f7b7dc5db5db :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895,
                                   id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription "We offer paid services to help the elderly live independently in their own home.  Nail cutting, Home Help, personal care.  Our voluntary Services include befriending, dog walking, gardening, benefit checks for those of retirement age, help to order and complete the appropriate claim forms. ";
                            :organisationName "Assist Teignbridge";
                            :organisationWebsite <www.https://www.assist-teignbridge.co.uk/>.
id:64182903012adbe2a8288921 :activityDescription """We offer two types of befriending service.  Telephone befriending involves calling your clients once (or twice) a week and having a good chat.  
Face to face is visiting your client once a week at a regular time to suit you.  You could meet up with your client  at home for a chat.  You could meet up for a coffee  in a cafe,  or accompany them to an event.  Our befrienders are a point of information for our clients by liaising with the Volunteer Coordinator.
You will need to provide 2 referees and undergo a DBS check, which the charity will pay for.
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64182575c656f7b7dc5db5db;
                            :activityTitle "Befriending via telephone or face to face".
id:64182dfb7bc87db9676f03e3 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """Launched in 1900, Urban Saints exists to reach young people in every community in the UK and Ireland. A Christian charity, we believe that groups led by volunteers who are sacrificially committed, passionate, and dedicated to young people, can truly change lives. There are no short-cuts to transforming a life or a culture. A young person’s life cannot be microwaved together in 3 minutes. It requires years of loving care and attention to see a young person grow, thrive, and flourish. 

Urban Saints recruits, trains, and supports volunteers to create  safe and welcoming places where young people can meet with their friends and be supported by caring adults. All groups carry the DNA of our Crusader roots, which seeks to reach young people – especially those who are disengaged from the church – recognising that relationships are more important than programmes as we walk alongside them on their faith journeys.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/a6e4c914-030b-446b-b21d-ac542611eb23_2023-03-20T09:57:14.659Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Urban Saints";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.urbansaints.org/>.
id:6418308548beab57a5aa8cdb :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """St Helena Hospice provides specialist palliative and end of life care to local people facing incurable illness in north east Essex, supporting them, their families, friends and carers.

We provide individual care and total support wherever it is needed; at home, over the phone with our 24/7 advice line, and at the hospice.

We also help adults in north Essex to cope with grief following the death of a loved one, regardless of where or how they died.

We reach out to members of our community, helping them make their own choices and live with dignity. By focusing on their physical, emotional and spiritual needs, we aim to bring comfort and relief to all those who need it, offering the people of north east Essex individual care and total support, regardless of their diagnosis or personal circumstances.

We rely heavily on the help and generosity of our fundraisers, donors and volunteers. Without them we wouldn’t be here.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/35c89323-912f-44bc-98ba-1bb341388db5_2023-03-20T10:08:04.753Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "St Helena Hospice";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.sthelena.org.uk/home>.
id:641834604d6b36449eeff79a :activityDescription """Dawlish Transport require Volunteer drivers to drive their specially adapted vehicles.  You can also use your own vehicle in some circumstances.  Hospital runs, lifts to attend appointments with GP's, anything goes.  The tasks may include using lifts to give access to vehicles for wheel chair users.
Our office is at the far end of Barton Hill Car park.
Free training given.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64182575c656f7b7dc5db5db;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteer Drivers".
id:641834de11ac4e5fee26ed1e :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897;
                            :organisationDescription "HVC was set up to facilitate volunteering in our community, open up the opportunities to become involved and engage local people to help local people, other charities and organisations. HVC champions the voluntary sector and volunteering by connecting, representing and supporting voluntary organisations. Our services include training, consultancy, information, resources and toolkits on governance, campaigning, strategy, impact and volunteer recruitment and retention. HVC will never underestimate the importance or power of volunteering in the community and strives to change the perception and understanding of what it means to be a volunteer.  Volunteering helps people’s health and mental wellbeing and helps organisation deliver their services with the support from volunteers.  HVC is the organisation that orchestrates this effectively and resourcefully. Never has volunteering been more needed than now to support our community in need. We have hundreds of meaningful volunteering roles for people to engage with, helping your community, helping you too as we know volunteering breaks down isolation, barriers and makes new friends.";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/81c391ea-9028-414e-b2c3-25d63a5ac86e_2023-03-20T10:26:37.830Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "HAVERING VOLUNTEER CENTRE HVC";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.haveringvc.org.uk/>.
id:64183a63685ab4e440f10c5e :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription "Unfold is charity organisation who through the support of amazing volunteers, provide 1-1 mentoring for children and young people between the ages 10-25 and for mothers with children aged 5+. Our method of mentoring is based on a psychological approach where we empower our mentees to get to where they want to be through achieving our aims of boosting Confidence, building lasting Independence and Connecting mentees with 1 or more organisation.  ";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/aead59d9-2133-4860-8629-29dd097648e4_2023-03-20T10:50:09.320Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Unfold";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.unfold.org.uk/>.
id:64183b9aef7f410ae8dd9abf :activityDescription """Unfold are currently looking for volunteers who can mentor on either our Mentoring for Mothers programme (female mentors only) or our Mentoring for Children and young people’s programme (female and male mentors) both of which have an adaptive programme to support asylum seekers. 

On both programmes you will: 
Over 12 to 24 weeks, support your mentee to help them achieve their goals and get them where they want to be through encouraging your mentee to consider future options and assist them in developing strategies to attain their goals. 

 Develop a trusting, supportive and non-judgemental relationship, empowering mentees to identify goals they wish to achieve.  

 Provide emotional, social and practical support to your mentee 

Support your mentee to make social connections with their local community, services or groups.  

 """;
                            :activityOrganisation id:64183a63685ab4e440f10c5e;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteers Mentors Needed to provide 1-2-1 Mentoring for Children and Young People and Mothers ".
id:64183e4f685ab4e440f1115d :activityDescription """Could you make a difference? We believe you can!

‘This is the one place I feel that I can be loved and accepted because I can’t do this at home.’ 
J, age 16 

Do you have skills in administration? Could you give some time each month to supporting local groups to help young people like 'J' find an exciting and safe place to belong and feel accepted? Our groups are run by volunteers who are supported by local leadership teams to reach the children and young people in their communities. Being an administration volunteer is extremely rewarding, both for you the volunteer and the group leaders and young people you support. Join us and be part of the Urban Saints family. 

You can do this from your own home, in your own time, and our Impact Team Leaders can support you with the templates and training you'll need. There is no time limit on what you give, but we recommend 4 hours a month could make a huge difference to the groups in your region.

Interested in knowing more about Urban Saints? Visit www.urbansaints.org.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64182dfb7bc87db9676f03e3;
                            :activityTitle "Admin Volunteer".
id:641843e14df4e3ad996e423c :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """Approach Dementia Support has been supporting those affected by dementia since 1981. Our charity provides services to support the well-being of people living with dementia, their families and carers.

We believe that everyone affected by dementia should be supported to live their life the way they choose and our wide range of specialist services allow us to assist our local communities throughout Staffordshire.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/a48f9fd4-bb62-442d-a5a6-ed642eedd252_2023-03-20T11:30:41.002Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Approach Dementia Support";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://approachstaffordshire.co.uk/>.
id:641860bfb5a1a53b5471a005 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """Central Yorkshire Scouts provide fun and friendship, challenge and everyday adventure to over 7,000 young people in Leeds and Wakefield, that’s boys and girls aged 6 to 25 from all cultures and backgrounds.

Scouting is all about learning whilst having fun and offers over 200 different activities. Young people are encouraged to stretch themselves, do their best, discover new talents and reach their potential. This may be during a weekly meeting participating in a challenging activity, discussing individual rights, investigating healthy lifestyles or working in the community or it could be during one of the over 26,000 night’s away adventures our Scouts enjoy every year.

Scouting’s approach is informal, youth-shaped and sociable; it is an enjoyable hobby and a social life with a purpose. Central Yorkshire Scouts help young people and adults in Leeds and Wakefield get to know new people, work well together and develop positive meaningful friendships.

Like the idea? Then why not find out more? Contact helen.thomas@scouts.org.uk or 07523 300811""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/c4a310d8-5d6c-4c80-9a25-c9415e1999fd_2023-03-21T12:06:22.760Z.png>;
                            :organisationName "Central Yorkshire Scouts";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.cycscouts.org.uk/>.
id:641863169f414d3c828ef519 :activityDescription """Want to take part in dementia research? Volunteers, both with and without dementia, who take part in studies or clinical trials play an essential role in dementia research.

alzheimersresearchuk.org/research/getting-involved-in-research

The best way to find out about how you can take part in dementia research studies is through a nationwide register called Join Dementia Research. When you register, you provide details about your health, age and contact preferences. This information is used to match you to studies that you are suitable to take part in, both online and face to face, helping researchers find volunteers for their studies.

Anyone over the age of 18, with or without a dementia diagnosis can sign-up to take part. Join Dementia Research is not a research study itself but a place to find out what studies you can take part in and enrol to take part. Signing up to the register is not a commitment to take part in any particular study, it just allows research studies to find willing volunteers.

alzheimersresearchuk.org/research/getting-involved-in-research""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:640f55f6185ebeaed23d95e4;
                            :activityTitle "Join Dementia Research | Alzheimer's Research UK".
id:641879d4cf71f77116117e87 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """Our vision is a world where diabetes can do no harm.

We’re leading the fight against the UK's biggest and growing health crisis. And it's a fight that involves us all – sharing knowledge and taking on diabetes together.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/8e343d1a-2456-4848-bd8b-6c61b8b5ac4e_2023-03-20T15:20:52.462Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Diabetes UK";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.diabetes.org.uk/>.
