id:670918baba4c9e15cc65c9d0 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """
The Say Aphasia charity provides support groups across the UK for people with aphasia; a language and communication disability caused by a brain injury like a stroke. Aphasia affects over 350,000 people in the UK but is little understood or known about by the public. It is the only aphasia charity set up by people with aphasia for people with aphasia; helping them to adapt to their new way of life and regain their independence and confidence.
Aphasia occurs due to injury to the brain such as a stroke. It affects access to language, resulting in difficulties following conversations, speaking, reading and writing. People’s language can be affected to different degrees so not everyone’s aphasia looks the same.  Imagine for a moment not being able to verbally order a coffee or ask for a bus ticket. This may be what life could look like for someone with aphasia. Aphasia is a lifelong condition and means that the person living with aphasia, their friends and family will need to adjust to a new way of communicating to overcome barriers. This may mean having to write or draw things instead of speaking. Due to the damage in the brain, people with aphasia may find it difficult to process conversions as quickly as before. Aphasia frequently results in loss of employment and a reduced social network, leading to isolation for the person living with aphasia.
Say Aphasia are always looking to welcome new volunteers to help out at the drop-in groups. 

Volunteering for the charity is very rewarding. The training and support ensures that the volunteers learn and enjoy their time with the charity. By giving your time to people with aphasia, you will be improving their quality of life. Many of the members attending the groups live alone, or have minimal social interactions. Having a regular drop-in group to meet others with similar experiences of living with aphasia, vastly improves their mental health and well being. As a volunteer, you can help the members to communicate with each other, help to organise activities, or help out with teas and coffees. Say Aphasia offers a warm welcome to volunteers. To be part of the team, contact hello@sayaphasia.org or fill in the application form https://www.sayaphasia.org/volunteer""";
                            :organisationName "Say Aphasia";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.sayaphasia.org/>.
id:670d3d31919ca4dbd11aa03d :activityDescription """London Open Gardens (LOG) is an annual event organised by London Parks & Gardens, a registered charity and the county Gardens Trust for London. LPG seeks to celebrate, champion, and cultivate London's green spaces as a trusted voice representing parks and gardens through the planning system.

This much-loved event began with volunteers’ passion to open up iconic but locked London squares in the west, and has grown to embrace 100+ gardens across central London including exquisite roof spaces, historic institutions, community allotments and reclaimed and rewilded urban oases. 

The Weekend is organised by LPG staff and Trustees with the support of a committed group of volunteers – the LPG Delivery Group. As a Garden Coordinator you will join this group. You will be expected to keep in regular contact with a selection of gardens in particular geographical area across London and oversee their involvement in LOG. You will also look for new and exciting gardens (not usually open to the public) to add to the list of participating gardens. 

We will contact you with a full role outline if you are interested in this role.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6425fe29f2d4b77a6e166bd1;
                            :activityTitle "Garden Coordinator - London Open Gardens 2026";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:670e5206b2387ed3e2c907e5 :activityDescription """Do you have a love of reading? 
Could you share that love with children in a local primary school?
Can you make a weekly afternoon commitment?

If you answered yes to these questions; we need you!

Coram Beanstalk has a long standing history of training volunteers to go into local primary schools and support children to grow a love of reading. 
You will be trained how to not just listen to children read, but also how to engage them in conversation about books and how to encourage them to find books they love. 

We provide training in small, online, interactive group sessions with one of our trainers as well as providing ongoing support once you are placed in school.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6425d5f5a0624f3ab9fbc763;
                            :activityTitle "Coram Beanstalk: In person reading help in school";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:670e5206b2387ed3e2c907e6 :address "Marmont Rd, London SE15 5TD, UK";
                            :latitude "51.4762139"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-0.0657436"^^xsd:double.
