id:680fa095e28c0671480a018d :activityDescription """Bright Futures UK is looking for passionate and engaging tutors to provide educational support to primary aged pupils in Maths and English and a fun, safe learning environment for young people living with long-term illnesses. 
 
What are we looking for? 
Subject Knowledge: Strong understanding of Primary Maths and English Curriculum. 
Teaching Skills: Ability to explain complex ideas in a clear and engaging way.  
Empathy and Patience: Kind and understanding nature, especially towards those facing health challenges.  
Reliability: Consistent availability for the agreed-upon time commitment.  
You must be a UK resident and be at least 18 years old.
 
What difference will you make? 
Academic Progress: Help young people stay on track with their studies despite their health challenges, ensuring they do not fall behind.  
Confidence Builder: Provide a stable and encouraging presence that helps build the students' confidence and resilience.  
Future Opportunities: Equip students with the knowledge and skills they need to succeed. 



As a charity that supports children and young people, we make safeguarding individuals our top priority. For this reason, all volunteers are subject to an enhanced criminal record and disclosures check. We also ask all volunteers to adhere to our Safeguarding, Equality and Confidentiality policies. These are nothing to worry about and if you have any questions, our team are happy to support you.
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6641f23d290ed27182315c83;
                            :activityTitle "Primary Maths and English Tutor for a Young Person with Serious Illness ";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:6810d842422c674f7b0e4f97 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """No matter how bad things are, we can help people severely affected by mental illness to improve their lives. We’re Rethink Mental Illness, a leading charity provider of mental health services in England.

People with experience of mental illness are at the heart of everything we do. They shape our expert advice, information and training,  our 300 services and groups, and they drive our campaigning to change the law and tackle discrimination.

We know that people severely affected by mental illness can have a good quality of life. Our work to achieve this is guided by seven core values:

Commitment.  We work tirelessly to provide support for everyone severely affected by mental illness. 

Equity. We believe that in a world where discrimination and disadvantage exist treating people with equity is critical to ensure justice and fairness for all.

Expertise. We constantly use our expertise to provide practical and personal support for people who are severely affected by mental illness.

Hope. We offer hope of a better quality of life for all those severely affected by mental illness.

Openness. We are open and transparent in all our work with beneficiaries, supporters, partners and the public to achieve change for people severely affected by mental illness. 

Passion. We are passionate about leading the way to a better quality of life for everyone severely affected by mental illness.

Understanding. People who are severely affected by mental illness are at the heart of everything we do in our organisation – our membership, our governance and our workforce.
""";
                            :organisationName "Rethink Mental illness";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.rethink.org/>.
id:6811f947d90e5c897cdd1fa7 :activityDescription """Join our movement of everyday heroes fighting for better mental health. As a Community Champion Volunteer, you’ll raise awareness, fundraise, and inspire others to support people living with mental illness.

It’s simple to get started:

- Sign up and receive your free fundraising page.

- Start fundraising – share your story, set a target, and track your progress.

- Make a difference – every penny raised helps fund peer-support groups across the country.

You’ll have access to everything you need, including a fundraising toolkit, digital resources, 1-to-1 support, and exclusive volunteer updates.

Rethink Mental Illness doesn’t receive government funding for its peer support groups — these lifelines only exist because of people like you. By becoming a Community Champion, you’ll help ensure no one has to face mental illness alone.

Whether you host an event, take on a personal challenge, or simply share our message, you’ll be making a lasting impact in your community.

It only takes 2 minutes to sign up – are you ready to champion mental health? If you’re interested, visit fundraising.rethink.org/community-champion to set up your page today.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6810d842422c674f7b0e4f97;
                            :activityTitle "Become a Community Champion Volunteer – Flexible & Ongoing";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68122110d2af80631d821bb5 :activityDescription """Do you have a love of reading? 
Could you share that love with children in a local primary school?
Can you make a weekly afternoon commitment?

If you answered yes to these questions; we need you!

Coram Beanstalk has a long standing history of training volunteers to go into local primary schools and support children to grow a love of reading. 
You will be trained how to not just listen to children read, but also how to engage them in conversation about books and how to encourage them to find books they love. 

We provide training in small, online, interactive group sessions with one of our trainers as well as providing ongoing support once you are placed in school.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6425d5f5a0624f3ab9fbc763;
                            :activityTitle "Coram Beanstalk: In person reading help in school";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68122110d2af80631d821bb6 :address "Charlwood Rd, London SW15 1PN, UK";
