id:685ad028ffa25e0d83f045dd :activityDescription """Do you believe that no one in Newport should have to call the streets ‘their home’ and that everyone deserves the right to a safe, secure, and settled home?

If yes, we are the opportunity for you!

Homelife - Citadel Newport is a community-based, volunteer-led support service, designed to help end homelessness and make a real difference to the lives of those who need it most.

Our volunteers help individuals both emotionally and practically in making the move to safe and secure housing. This can include finding and settling into a new home, setting up utilities and bills, sourcing furniture and decorating. Volunteers also help individuals to connect and engage with relevant support providers and networks to help ensure new tenancies are a success.

Our support can be as simple as having a coffee, providing a listening ear, and letting an individual know that someone cares. Our volunteers also bring us ideas, and experiences to shape how we make a difference.

We are currently recruiting local volunteers and sponsors and want to reach out to all sectors of the community. Is there something you can do to help and be part of a solution?

We offer a variety of opportunities to get involved, from 1:1 volunteer support work, fundraising and collecting donations to helping arrange activities and events for those accessing our services or volunteering

Volunteers are offered a full induction, interesting training, ongoing supervision, and a chance to make a real difference in the community whilst meeting likeminded, caring people.

All we ask is that you can volunteer an hour or more a week, for up to 6 months, and that you take part in our induction, training, and support.

To find out more please contact Cath on Homelife@housingjustice.org.uk or apply here.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6830766bfba6c824a07fbb65;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteer for Homelife Project - Newport";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:685ad028ffa25e0d83f045de :address "Newport, RI, USA";
                            :latitude "41.491756"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-71.3118637"^^xsd:double.
id:685b6678e26f102460f079fb :activityDescription """The RSPB manages a big network of nature reserves which are havens for wildlife, and importantly very special places for people of all ages and abilities to visit and experience a nature rich landscape.  

Nature is in trouble, there aren't many places left where wildlife can thrive, that is why we want to welcome more people to these places. 

We are looking for volunteers that can enhance the enjoyment of RSPB Lochwinnoch for all visitors, from all user groups, of all ages and all levels of experience/knowledge of nature, using positive face-to-face engagement with visitors. This role is suitable for individuals or couples. You must be willing to spend time out on site, wandering the trails, talking to all types of visitors. Providing assistance and encouragement to visitors less experienced in bird and wildlife watching, as well as engaging with more experienced visitors to discuss their findings and share information. Where appropriate and safe, to deal with any misuse of the site by members of the public and provide guidance in proper use to visitors. Monitor site for any issues and report them to staff. Encouraging visitors to offer their feedback.

Full training and support will be provide for this role but you do need to be a ‘people person’ and have good communication skills. 

This is a really rewarding and enjoyable role. You will be working with a friendly team on a beautiful nature reserve, you will have the opportunity to make new friends and spend time outdoors. You will get to meet loads of new people in this role, enjoying conversation and helping people have a wonderful visit. 

There is always the opportunity to learn new things and gain skills through volunteering, by working on a reserve, you'll naturally learn all about how nature reserves are managed and the wildlife that is found there. 

We are looking for individuals or couples than can offer at least one half day per week on a fixed rota system

Thank you for reading this role description. Clicking on ‘more about this role’ and the downloadable pdf will give you just the generic role profile for this opportunity. A role profile is the family to which an opportunity belongs and informs vetting and training requirements and may not be an exact fit to the role described above""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Visitor Engagement Volunteer (On Reserves) (Visitor Engagement - Outdoors)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:685b6678e26f102460f079fc :address "Lochwinnoch, Largs Road, Lochwinnoch, Renfrewshire, PA12 4JF, Scotland";
                            :latitude "55.788571"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-4.618754"^^xsd:double.
id:685b6679e26f102460f07a20 :activityDescription """Can you help us make a difference to bees and butterflies as well as providing great spaces for families to explore? 

The RSPB and Ella’s Kitchen are working together to restore wildflower meadows across the UK, and at Dove Stone we are running regular events to engage families and children in activities to help pollinators and increase biodiversity.  We are looking for people who can help us to run these events, which may include wildflower planting and creative activities themed around pollinators.

Tasks will include:
• Welcoming people to site and directing them to where an event or activity is taking place
• Assisting the project staff with putting up signage; handing out gloves and tools; showing people how to plant flowers or assisting with other related activities
• Litter picking around the planting area and route to it if needed
• Helping to tidy up at the end of a session

Full training and support will be provided for this role but you do need the following skills and experience:

- You must be a ‘people person’ - a confident, engaging communicator who enjoys meeting new people, especially families and young people (which may include school or nursery children and brownies, cubs etc)
- Organisational skills, to help ensure events and activities are carried out safely
-An interest in learning a little about pollinators and grassland management
- A willingness to muck in and help with whatever activity is being run that day
- Physically fit enough to walk around site, help load and unload van and carry kit to the event area.

This is a really rewarding and enjoyable role. You will be working with friendly staff on a beautiful site, with the opportunity to spend time outdoors and contribute to an important conservation project. You will get to meet loads of new people, helping them to help nature and have a wonderful visit.

There is always the opportunity to learn new things and gain skills through volunteering, by working outside on a site like this, you'll naturally learn all about how nature reserves are managed and the wildlife that is found there. Travel expenses are available up to a maximum of 25 miles per day.

Events take place at various times including term time, school holidays, some weekends and some evenings. Events may not happen weekly.

(Please note Dove Stone is quite an open, exposed site - there is no visitor centre or cafe and the Council run toilet block is some distance from where the events usually take place)""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Visitor Engagement Family and Children Events (Wildflower Planting Events Volunteer)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:685b6679e26f102460f07a21 :address "Dove Stone, Bank Lane, Greenfield, Yorkshire West Riding, OL3 7NE, England";
                            :latitude "53.526801"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.979957"^^xsd:double.
