id:68707e889d85a6e0b1cb51b3 :activityDescription """The RSPB manages a big network of nature reserves which are havens for wildlife, and importantly very special places for people of all ages and abilities to visit and experience a nature rich landscape.  

Nature is in trouble, there aren't many places left where wildlife can thrive, that is why we want to welcome more people to these places. 

We are looking for volunteers who can help with all our visitor engagement activities,  specifically welcoming people to our Reserve.

As a Visitor Engagement Volunteer at Rainham Marshes the tasks you will undertake are:

- Providing a friendly welcome to visitors when they arrive and making them aware of parking/entry requirements.
- Having conversations with visitors and finding out how you can help them enjoy their visit, whether that's pointing out where they can see wildlife.
- Talking to the public about the work of the RSPB, what we're all about and asking them if they would like to become members if the conversation takes you there.  
- Ensuring the welcome area is clean and tidy, leaflets are replenished, and information boards are up to date.

Full training and support will be provided for this role but if you enjoy nature and talking to people then this is the role for you.

Any hours that you are able to give us throughout the week will be welcomed, but we are also looking for volunteers who would be happy to cover our Welcome desk on Saturdays, whether its one Saturday a month or every Saturday. 

This is a really rewarding and enjoyable role. You will be working with a friendly team on a beautiful nature reserve, you will have the opportunity to make new friends and spend time outdoors. You will get to meet loads of new people in this role, enjoying conversation and helping people have a wonderful visit. 

There is always the opportunity to learn new things and gain skills through volunteering, by working on a reserve, you'll naturally learn all about how nature reserves are managed and the wildlife that is found there.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Visitor Engagement Volunteer (On Reserves) (Welcome Volunteer)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68707e889d85a6e0b1cb51b4 :address "Rainham Marshes, New Tank Hill Road, Purfleet, Essex, RM19 1SZ, England";
                            :latitude "51.486792"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "0.227471"^^xsd:double.
id:68707e889d85a6e0b1cb51d8 :activityDescription "Why not come and join us to sit and collect observational data at Walton on the Naze in Essex. This research assistant post requires no previous knowledge or training. Our project aims to develop and test superior signs to safeguard endangered birds from disturbances from dog walking. We need your help to test the effectiveness of these new signs by observing dog walker behaviour on a beautiful beach in Essex. You won’t be challenging or confronting anyone, just sitting quietly and recording a few notes on how well the signs seem to be working; and of course enjoying your surroundings. Feel free to sign up with a friend so that you can have a natter while you gather data that will help safeguard vulnerable birds.";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "RSPB Team Member (Research assistant (no training/experience required) at Walton on the Naze Essex)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68707e889d85a6e0b1cb51d9 :address "Old Hall Lane, CO14 8LE, Old Hall Lane, Walton on the Naze, Essex, CO14 8LE, United Kingdom";
                            :latitude "51.862163"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "1.286073"^^xsd:double.
id:68707e898d829313a233880c :activityDescription """**You will be sharing bedrooms with other people of the same gender. You will need to tick 'willing to share' for your application to be processed.**

**Why We Want You:** 

This is a unique chance to be part of one of the most ambitious projects to save seabirds across the UK.

Rathlin Island has the largest seabird breeding colony in Northern Ireland, including Puffins, Razorbills, and the largest population of Guillemots across the whole of the UK and Ireland. Their future is threatened, however, by invasive non-native species, namely rats and ferrets. Last year only one in three Puffin chicks survived long enough to leave the nest.

The LIFE Raft project aims to protect these seabirds by eradicating the rats and ferrets, and this volunteering opportunity is critical to making this project a success.

Last September we placed about 6,000 bait stations across the island. These were baited with rodenticide, which were then removed and baited again with wax blocks for monitoring until April this year.  The bait stations have remained in situ and volunteers will be working as part of a wider team that check and rebait each station every two weeks.

**What’s In It For You**

As an RSPB volunteer you’ll be part of something big – a team of amazing and dedicated people who donate their time, energy, talent and skills to help birds and other wildlife.

Rathlin is a beautiful island with a close-knit community of about 150 people. There is a hotel, a cooperatively run store, shops with local products, a pub and café, and regular yoga and film nights and other community events.

There is also lots for the wildlife enthusiast. While the seabirds are a strong draw in the spring and the summer, there are oystercatchers, eider ducks and seals all year round, and dolphins are regularly seen from the ferries and harbours.

Rathlin’s dark skies also allow for stunning views of the stars. 2024 proved to be a particularly good year for the northern lights, it’s a great time to be spending time on island.

Accommodation is provided in a hostel just half a mile from the harbour, or in an old cottage about five kilometres inland. You will be sharing a room with members of the same gender

**Requirements**

**Minimum age 18**. 
Enthusiasm and ability to work both on your own initiative and as part of a team are necessities – but perhaps most importantly, you must be good with people and physically fit and active.  

Please take into account that volunteers hike over rough and varied terrain in the winter, through all weathers. It also requires lots of working with your hands in the cold, and wearing gloves is not always possible due to the nature of the work. It is physically tough but incredibly rewarding.

There are about 150 people who live on Rathlin Island and volunteers get to know people well over the course of the programme. You will be informal ambassadors for the project, even while off-duty, and it is expected that you will be respectful and considerate of the community at all times.

**Notes**

There is no charge to take part in the scheme. All accommodation and a contribution to food (sufficient to cover three meals a day) will be provided, as well as initial travel to Rathlin from anywhere in the UK or Ireland. Travel on and off the island at the weekends is not covered, and as it is dependent on the weather and ferry availability, should be undertaken only occasionally. We are not able to cover the cost of off-island accommodation or other expenses should you be unable to return due to ferry cancellations.

**Volunteers will be expected to volunteer 5-day weeks** 

**UPDATE   - we have several training start dates : - 
1. 15th September - minimum 4 weeks,
2. 13th October - minimum 6 weeks,
3. 24th November - minimum 4-6 weeks. 
4. 5th January 2026 - minimum 6 weeks
5. 16th February 2026 - minimum 6 weeks to the end of March 2026
you can come for the minimum amount of time, but to get the most out of the experience we recommend you stay for as long as you are able.   

No pets allowed - due to the nature of the work

**UPDATE - if you do not have a driving licence please provide other photographic evidence, for example a passport, when you are asked to download a copy of your driving licence**

Below are a couple of quotes from last years volunteers: - 

“Take the leap, you just don’t know where it’s going to take you. Maybe it will take you somewhere you never imagined, but somewhere better than where you thought you’d be. You’ll learn lots of important skills – which is obvious enough. But the experience, the people you meet, the connections you make. It was an amazing experience – one of the best things I’ve done in my life.”
**Frances Avenell, Residential volunteer – LIFE Raft 2024-25**

“It’s a really good step in a conservation career. If you can do a winter on Rathlin, then it demonstrates that you’re going to have no problems when it comes to applying to be a Seabird Warden on a remote island in the UK – or anywhere else in the world really. I think it’s going to have given everyone an enormous CV boost.”
**Julie Hunt, Residential volunteer – LIFE Raft 2024-25**""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Residential on Reserve (More than 4 weeks) (LIFE Raft Fieldworker Volunteer (Rathlin))";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68707e898d829313a233880d :address "Rathlin West Light Seabird Centre, Rathlin West Light Seabird Centre, Rathlin Ireland, Ballycastle, Co Antrim, BT54 6RT, United Kingdom";
                            :latitude "55.293389"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-6.197745"^^xsd:double.
id:6870d22d24c63d1223ac1e77 :activityDescription """Retail & Furniture Assembly Volunteers  - A New Volunteer Opportunity in Acorns Children's Hospice Superstore in Tewkesbury  

Acorns have a network of shops across the West Midlands that generate a substantial level of income to enable us to care for children and their families. 

To help us to continue to provide the care that we do, we are looking for fun and enthusiastic volunteers to join us in our Tewkesbury Superstore. Our shops are exciting and dynamic, with no two days being the same. From our walk-in customers, to receiving donations, and everything in between. the store is open Monday to Saturday 9am to 5pm
Sunday 10am to 4pm

What does volunteering involve?

Shop Volunteer

Engaging with customers
Accepting and sorting donated stock
Pricing donations for sale
Working on the till
Furniture Assembly Volunteer

Building and dismantling flat pack furniture
Working within timeframes for customer assembly requests
Flagging defects or spare parts needed when constructing furniture
Carrying out minor repairs on donated goods
How much time will it take? 

The shop will be open Monday to Sunday, 9am to 6pm. You can volunteer the hours that suit you, but ideally we’d like you to volunteer for at least 3 hours per week.

Why volunteer at Acorns? 

To help a local charity and make a difference in your local community
Connect with likeminded people and make new friends
Enhance your CV by learning new skills and gaining experience
Opportunities to progress to other roles
Access to free online courses
Retail discounts
Invites to social events and participation in our volunteer recognition schemes and rewards
Full training will be provided

 """;
                            :activityOrganisation id:641096af1097b43af15de362;
                            :activityTitle "Retail and Furniture Volunteer (Tewkesbury Superstore)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:6870d22d24c63d1223ac1e78 :address "High Street, Tewkesbury GL20 5JY";
                            :latitude "51.9946142"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.1565282"^^xsd:double.
id:6870e89314c3a79dbb756baa :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """We want to work with forward thinking and socially responsible employers to help us to build the first national resource helping disadvantaged students to connect with large employers, and impact social mobility at a national level.

We need you to provide both ‘world of work interactions’ through 2 hour workplace visits for this cohort of young people and also to help us reach students in all parts of the England with high social deprivation.

These young people are far more likely to miss out on early exposure to the world of work. They need big employers to proactively include and invite them to interact.
By providing meaningful interactions for disadvantaged young people from age 11, you can easily and quickly influence large numbers of students using our existing network.

You can help us by :
1. Providing 2-hour workplace visits across a number of sites. A workplace visit is for 5 students along with their teacher, it’s a welcome, overview of who the organisation and what they do, the types of jobs roles that exist within the company, a site tour, an activity session and a Q&A

2. Going in to local schools to talk either one of two things:

      * your organisation – who you are and what you do

     * Support the existing school career curriculum with things like CV wring, mock interviews, interview techniques, career fairs. 
""";
                            :organisationName "Ductu".
id:6870ea56dec68c7f67fca5e6 :activityDescription """We're inviting you to make a meaningful difference in the lives of young people by volunteering at a local school. This opportunity is part of your Organisations commitment to giving back to the community and supporting students aged 11–19 years of age and are neurodivergent, have Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND), Additional Learning Needs (ALN), receive free school meals, or attend alternative provision settings.

As a volunteer, you’ll help bring careers education to life. You can either:
•	Introduce Students to our Industry: Share who we are, what we do, and the wide range of job opportunities within our organisation. Your personal career journey could be the spark that helps a student see new possibilities for their future.
•	Support Career Readiness: Help students build essential skills by taking part in mock interviews, CV writing sessions, or other career-focused activities as part of the school's curriculum.

No prior experience working with young people is necessary—just a willingness to listen, share, and encourage. Every interaction can make a lasting impact, helping students build confidence, raise aspirations, and better understand the world of work.

Join us in shaping brighter futures—your time and experience could change a young person’s life.

Getting Started is Easy
If you're interested in volunteering, Ductu aim to match you with schools within an 8-mile radius of your location, but are happy to adjust this based on your travel preferences.
To list your opportunity and connect you with a suitable school, we’ll just need a few details from you:
•	Your postcode – To find nearby schools (this is not published)
•	Contact details – So that Ductu can arrange directly with you.
•	Preferred activity – Whether you'd like to deliver an employer insight session, support the careers curriculum, or both.
•	Availability – Let us know when you're generally free (months, days, and morning/ afternoon).
•	Session length – How much time you can commit per session (e.g., 2, 4, 6, or 8 hours).
Once we have this information, Ductu will reach out to local schools and coordinate all the details with you when a match is made.
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6870e89314c3a79dbb756baa;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteer in local schools to support 11-19 yr olds who are neurodiverse, SEND, on free school meals, or attend alternative provision schooling.";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:6870ede8a178ed8bad7c54c8 :activityDescription """Please advise if your site can host 2 hour workplace visits for students aged 11-19 who are neurodiverse, SEND, on free school meals, or attend alternative provision schooling

We ask for:
•	3 visits per site per year 
•	For 5 students plus a teacher for each visit. Schools are generally within 8 mile radius
•	A Workplace visit includes a welcome to Mitie, types of job roles (particulalry entry level roles), a tour, an activity session and a Q&A

If you want to register please let Cristina Fieraru know and we will include your location as an opportunity available to schools. """;
                            :activityOrganisation id:6870e89314c3a79dbb756baa;
                            :activityTitle "2 hour workplace visits for 5 students and their teacher - students aged 11-19, neurodiverse, SEND, on free school meals, or attend alternative provision schooling";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:6870f31642956ab2455c5bca :activityOrganisation id:65b7befedcc4366de5311230;
