id:68863036bcedd87cd67acbca :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50899;
                            :organisationDescription """At Hope Against Suicide, we do things differently. Our unique Mental Health Response Service meets people where they are, on the streets of Bristol, whether they’re shopping, grabbing food, or simply out for a walk.

You’ll spot our volunteers wearing identifiable uniform (a blue hi-vis vest marked Hope Against Suicide or Mental Health Responder), working in small teams across the city centre. They’re there in the moment, offering face-to-face mental health support without barriers or wait times. They specialise in face-to-face mental health and suicide first aid, offering immediate, compassionate, person-to-person interactions to those struggling with poor mental health.

We know that many people who are struggling won’t reach out to a helpline or book a GP appointment. That’s why being physically present matters. A simple chat, a smile, or a moment of connection can turn someone’s day, or even their life, around.

Our support includes community foot patrols, welfare check-in calls, and advocacy, making meaningful contributions to mental health and suicide prevention. We aim to assist people at the earliest signs of mental health decline, working to prevent crises before they escalate.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/19c2c4fa-bcb4-448c-8d1b-25df48e0df47_2025-07-27T13:57:10.199Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Hope Against Suicide";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://hopeagainstsuicide.org.uk/>.
id:68863315a2433179c3ae857c :activityDescription """Are you a natural organiser who wants to make a real difference?
We’re looking for someone really special here to join our leadership team: someone with specialist knowledge in operations, leadership experience, and strong people skills, who can support and lead our mental health response operations in the Bristol City Centre.
This is a volunteer position with real impact: you’ll be part of a small, agile team delivering frontline mental health support in public spaces, from gentle listening to fast, compassionate intervention in moments of crisis.
You will volunteer on the front lines but most importantly, supporting the amazing volunteers behind the scenes too.
You will oversee shift planning, and supporting team communication, enhancing our operational smoothness, seeking opportunities and actively problem solving. 
You’ll play a vital role making sure our shifts and volunteer team can function smoothly, working closely with the management team. 
We’re looking for someone with:
•	Operational or logistics experience (professional or voluntary)

•	Strong people skills and clear communication

•	A calm, problem-solving approach under pressure

•	A deep commitment to mental health support and suicide prevention

Your responsibilities will include:

•	Overseeing and improving shift planning & deployment

•	Ensuring operational safety, legality, and safeguarding compliance and the highest standard of care and safety. 

•	Supporting and coordinating volunteer teams on the ground

•	Identifying and addressing gaps, risks or needs in our delivery

•	Working closely with the charity’s leadership team to evolve and adapt our operations as we grow

We estimate around 4-6 voluntary hours a week are needed. Some of these hours will involve optional frontline shifts to help you stay grounded in the work.
You’ll be supported by a passionate and friendly team, and your work will directly help prevent suicide and provide frontline support to people who need a friendly face and a light conversation, to people in crisis requiring a quick acting compassionate response. 
You can be part of a critical stage of development, shaping how a grassroots response charity operates in real time to save lives and hold hope for people in their darkest moments.

""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68863036bcedd87cd67acbca;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteer Operations manager (Hope Against Suicide)- Bristol based. ";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68863315a2433179c3ae857d :address "The Arcade, Broadmead, Bristol BS1 3JA";
                            :latitude "51.457898"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.5904093"^^xsd:double.
id:6886348a632c99dbb7975fe3 :activityDescription """Location: Bristol (on-foot patrols and/or Listening Hub, near Cabot Circus)
Time Commitment: Minimum 2 patrol shifts per month (6 hours per month)
Term: Minimum 12 months
Reporting to: Response Manger 

About Hope Against Suicide
Hope Against Suicide is an award-winning Bristol based, volunteer-led mental health charity committed to preventing suicide and supporting community wellbeing. We provide emotional support and crisis response to those experiencing mental health struggles, whether through proactive street-based outreach or meaningful conversations in our Listening Hub.
Why the Team Lead Role Matters
The Team Lead plays a critical role in in ensuring our shifts run smoothly, safely, and compassionately. You will be the designated lead during your shift, responsible for overseeing a small team of 2–3 volunteers. Your role includes coordinating incident responses where required, ensuring best practices and de-escalation, and helping to maintain a calm, supportive environment for both the public and your team.
You’ll follow established protocols and processes while being empowered to make on the ground decisions and escalate situations appropriately. 

Key Responsibilities
•	Lead and support your team of 2–3 responders during each shift.
•	Engage with vulnerable individuals, including those in early stages of mental health decline or at risk of suicide. Support may range from having a friendly chat to carrying out crisis interventions.
•	Ensure shift data is anonymously recorded and shared with the Response Manager before the end of each shift.
•	Report incidents and risks in a timely manner.
•	Provide a pre-shift briefing and post-shift debrief to your team.
•	Ensure your team has everything they need to start the shift confidently.
•	Check in with your team after emotionally challenging shifts, prioritising volunteer wellbeing,
•	Model good practice in mental health response, in line with our values: hope, compassion, neutrality, inclusivity, and a people-first approach.
•	Attend mandatory training and participate in ongoing development to strengthen your leadership and frontline skills.

Qualities and Experience We’re Looking For
We welcome individuals from all backgrounds who are confident in offering support and capable of leading a team with kindness, clarity, and emotional intelligence.
You may have professional, voluntary or lived experience in one or more of the following areas:
•	Mental health support, suicide prevention, or crisis intervention
•	Counselling, social work, nursing, support work or peer support
•	Safeguarding and trauma-informed practice
•	Supporting individuals facing homelessness or social isolation
•	Managing emotionally charged or high-pressure situations
•	Leading frontline or operational teams in a similar environment

You will also bring:
•	Confidence in guiding others and making calm, informed decisions
•	Strong communication and team-building skills
•	A compassionate, non-judgemental and inclusive mindset
•	Willingness to escalate concerns and seek support when needed

Volunteer Requirements
•	Aged 18 or over
•	Able to commit to at least 6 hours (2 shifts) per month 
•	Willing to complete all relevant training
•	Comfortable leading others and responding to emotionally complex situations
•	Enhanced DBS check required (provided by us)
•	Access to a phone for shift communications
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68863036bcedd87cd67acbca;
                            :activityTitle "Mental Health Responder (Team Lead)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:6886348a632c99dbb7975fe4 :address "Broadmead, Bristol BS1 3BX";
                            :latitude "51.45851340000001"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.584557600000001"^^xsd:double.
id:688635f5cda03f3aff8e783c :activityDescription """About the Role

Hope Against Suicide is seeking a compassionate and committed Response Manager to help us deliver life-changing support in suicide prevention. This is a great opportunity to gain management experience, boost your CV, and make a real impact.
Our charity is run entirely by volunteers and has grown from an idea into a community-driven initiative supporting people in mental health crisis. We’re now looking for someone to help coordinate and strengthen our frontline response efforts in central Bristol.

Your Responsibilities
1. Service Oversight & Support
•	Join and complete training to fully understand our Mental Health Response model.
•	Support shift coordination and help manage the rota to ensure reliable service coverage.
•	Act as the first point of contact for our volunteer team, particularly our Team Leads.
•	Provide guidance and support to ensure volunteers feel valued and connected.
•	Attend at least one shift per week and support at an operational level.
2. Troubleshooting & Improvement
•	Gather and respond to volunteer and service user feedback.
•	Work with leadership to continuously improve our support services.
3. Volunteer Recruitment & Development
•	Support recruitment efforts and help onboard new volunteers.
•	Conduct interviews and ensure new team members align with our mission and values.
4. Community Engagement
•	Build relationships with local businesses and organisations during patrols.
•	Work closely with the Listening Hub Manager to ensure both services are aligned and complementary.
5. Operational Leadership
•	Monitor team and public safety during shifts, responding to concerns calmly and professionally.
•	Ensure team visibility and readiness to offer support to anyone in distress.
•	Follow risk assessments and safety protocols at all times.
6. Reporting
•	Provide updates to the CEO and Operations Manager.
•	Address any issues and contribute to strategic planning.
What We're Looking For
•	Commitment of 4–6 hours per week.
•	Strong communication, reliability, and organisational skills.
•	Passion for mental health and suicide prevention.
•	Confidence in working with volunteers and community stakeholders.
•	A willingness to learn and engage with training.
•	Ability to collaborate across teams and maintain accountability.
What You’ll Gain
•	A meaningful role with impact in your local community.
•	Qualifications including funded Suicide First Aid (after 3 months).
•	Development of valuable soft skills: communication, empathy, leadership.
•	Experience in the mental health and non-profit sectors.
•	A strong addition to your CV.
No prior mental health experience is needed—just compassion, commitment, and a team spirit. Uniform and training provided.

How to Apply
Please email your expression of interest to info@hopeagainstsuicide.org.uk with:
•	What interests you about this role?
•	Any relevant personal or professional experiences or skills.
•	How often (and for how many hours) you could volunteer per month.
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68863036bcedd87cd67acbca;
                            :activityTitle "Response Manager";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:688635f5cda03f3aff8e783d :address "Broadmead, Bristol BS1 3BX";
                            :latitude "51.45851340000001"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.584557600000001"^^xsd:double.
id:68863750cda03f3aff8e78b9 :activityDescription """Role Summary:

We’re looking for a warm, confident communicator who can represent our charity at local events, talks, stalls, and community spaces, and fundraising events when we’re asked to represent. This role is “as and when needed,” usually around 10 hours per month, with peaks during campaign periods or community outreach opportunities.
Perfect for someone who wants flexible, meaningful work connecting with people and raising awareness about suicide prevention and mental wellbeing.
Key Details:

•	Volunteer  Pattern: Ad hoc, mostly evenings or weekends at public events and community spaces
•	Location: In-person in Bristol area.
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68863036bcedd87cd67acbca;
                            :activityTitle "Community Engagement Officer (Flexible Hours)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68863750cda03f3aff8e78ba :address "Broadmead, Bristol BS1 3BX";
                            :latitude "51.45851340000001"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.584557600000001"^^xsd:double.
id:68863c648480b48234c1febe :activityDescription """About Us: 

Hope Against Suicide is a mental health charity committed to providing face-to-face support to individuals in our community. Operating primarily in Bristol, we focus on raising awareness and offering practical help for those struggling with mental health challenges. To learn more about our mission and services, visit our about page.

The Role:
We’re looking for enthusiastic and creative volunteers to join our Events and Fundraising team. As an Events Planner, you will play a key role in organising and executing events designed to raise awareness and funds for our cause.

This is a flexible, hybrid role, with most tasks able to be completed remotely as you plan for your event, however, attending events in person is essential to ensure everything runs smoothly. You will help plan events ranging from small, local gatherings to larger community-wide initiatives, as well as identifying external events we can participate in. Your work will support both our fundraising efforts and awareness campaigns.

Key Responsibilities:
Plan and organise events that raise awareness and funds for Hope Against Suicide.
Collaborate with the team to ensure events run smoothly and align with our charity’s goals.
Assist with creating and promoting event materials.
Manage event logistics and coordination, both in preparation and on the day of the event.
What We’re Looking For:
A creative, outgoing individual with a passion for mental health initiatives.
Strong organisational skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks effectively.
Someone who can take an idea or concept and bring it to life through successful event planning.
Ability to dedicate a few hours per week, with flexibility to fit your schedule""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68863036bcedd87cd67acbca;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteer Fundraiser/Events Planner";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68863c648480b48234c1febf :address "From Home";
