id:689215f100f28bd8090bcfbf :activityDescription """This is a pivotal volunteer-to-permanent position for someone with strong grant writing or fundraising strategy experience who is committed to our mission. You will lead the design and implementation of a sustainable grants funding strategy to cover our monthly operational and programme costs.

This is not a short-term, one-off bid-writing role. We’re seeking someone who sees this as a long-term journey. Once funding is secured, the role becomes a paid, full-time post with a competitive salary and creative freedom.

Key Responsibilities
Grant Strategy & Pipeline:

Design a monthly/quarterly grants calendar to secure consistent income
Research and apply for relevant UK-based grants from trusts, foundations, and public funds
Write compelling bids that reflect our mission and lived experiences
Maintain a funding tracker including deadlines and reporting requirements
Sustainable Income:

Secure multi-year and unrestricted core funding
Build a diversified funding portfolio
Support the CIC’s 12-month and 3-year financial roadmap
Relationship Management:

Build and maintain strong relationships with funders
Draft reports, budgets, and impact summaries
Contribute to community-led case studies and evaluation data

Collaboration:

Work alongside the Founder and Outreach & Partnerships lead
Attend monthly in-person London team meetings (refreshments/travel covered)
Share progress and funding milestones openly
*
*

Ideal Candidate
Proven success securing five- and six-figure grants
Understands core vs project funding and funder expectations
Familiar with funders like National Lottery, Arts Council, Paul Hamlyn, Esmée Fairbairn, City Bridge Trust, etc.
Passionate about racial justice, trauma recovery, and systemic change
Self-motivated and looking for a long-term principal role in a Black-led movement
Job Types: Full-time, Volunteer

Benefits:


Company events
Discounted or free food
Flexitime
Free parking
On-site parking
Work from home
Experience:


Grant writing: 3 years (required)
Work Location: Hybrid remote in Barnet EN4 9AT

Reference ID: Grants W

Expected start date: 20/08/2025""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68920e0bdf6bd09b7ae069a2;
                            :activityTitle "Strategic Grant Writer (Volunteer to Permanent)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:689215f100f28bd8090bcfc0 :address "From Home";
                            :latitude "52.5647271"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.4671882"^^xsd:double.
id:6892278907ce03c18ad97fbc :activityDescription """At St John Ambulance we are looking to embed ourselves within our communities, delivering support which is tailored to the needs of those communities whilst providing a positive experience for our Volunteers.

We need leaders who are passionate about making a difference in their communities, who care about helping and developing others, who will lead and inspire a volunteer movement across a geographical area to deliver Community First Aid, Community Education and Community Fundraising as well as develop our offering for young people. Through leading a team of Volunteer leaders, you will drive excellence in delivery, develop an inclusive and diverse volunteering experience for new and current volunteers, and help navigate through change to build a bigger and better St John Ambulance.

As a member of our network leadership team, you’ll get a huge sense of satisfaction and pride through supporting a team and our volunteers to deliver our essential services. It’s great for you, great for your community and great for our nation.

We will make sure you will have access to a proper induction to volunteering with SJA and the training you need for the role you take on. We will also provide you with a supportive environment while you are volunteering with us. Information about our volunteer roles are included in the role descriptions. What you can expect from SJA, and what we expect from those who volunteer with us, is included in the volunteer charter.

Volunteers would be expected to attend training for the role both online and in person, to attend regularly network weekly meetings to enable them to keep their skills up to date.

If you are successful you will need to undertake relevant training for the role, details of which will be discussed after you have been appointed.

Closing date for these opportunities is: 31/08/2025
If you are interested in this role then please contact paul.hannibal@sja.org.uk

To apply for this opportunity please follow the link below:""";
