id:68adf38e43d0618d8ef4af02 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a1;
                            :organisationDescription """In 1993, Daniel De-Gale was diagnosed with acute lymphoblastic leukaemia. He became the 1st black individual in the UK to receive a stem cell transplant from an unrelated donor. Unfortunately, due to complications with his health, Daniel passed in 2008. Daniel’s story could have been different if his match had been found sooner.

As a leading Blood cancer/disorder charity, the African Caribbean Leukaemia Trust is dedicated to raising awareness of the severe shortage of donors of Black heritage in the United Kingdom on the stem cell (bone marrow), blood, and organ donor registers.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/8700c6b8-1cac-41ad-98d2-a30b0bf27014_2025-08-26T17:49:01.275Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "ACLT ";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://aclt.org/>.
id:68adf7be557b9ee752c21962 :activityDescription """This September, the ACLT is premiering our “One Drop Part 2” – a short film set in a barbershop that tackles the stigma around donation among Black men. The event will be in London and we’re bringing together celebrities, influencers, community voices, sickle cell warriors, and a strong social media campaign to spark conversation and action.

Location: Patch London - (58-60 Carter Lane, London EC4V 5EF)
Time: 6:30 until later (the event starts at 7 pm)
Pay: London-based, will cover travel expenses

We are looking for two volunteer actors who want to be an interactive part of the night. You will engage with the attendees as your character, which fits the themes of our night, which is to tackle the myths and misconceptions surrounding blood donation. This will be a fun night and a fun role that you can make your own. 

We have created a fake newspaper, and the theme of the night is 'forget the fake news'. You will have a copy of this, so you know what the stories are inside this. 

We are looking for: 

1 x Black Male Barber (this is to fit with the theme of our short film, and your character will be based on someone in the short film). This character is stubborn and believes in conspiracies behind not donating blood. We have hired a barber's chair, which you can stand beside, and as people walk past, you will interact with them. 

1 x Detective Journalist - your role will be to go around the bar we have hired and talk to our guests, you will question them on blood donation and what you think about the latest news about the ACLT (us) and ask them questions and see if you can find out more about what the newspapers have been reporting. 

We will organise a call with you so you will know more about the night and the themes. If you are interested and want to know more, you can contact us. 
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68adf38e43d0618d8ef4af02;
                            :activityTitle "Interactive Actors for Short Film Premiere Night ";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68adf7be557b9ee752c21963 :address "58-60 Carter Lane, London EC4V 5EF";
                            :latitude "51.5133698"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-0.1020173"^^xsd:double.
id:68ae234e5b749350cdd0ec72 :cause id:64093b542eed5dcadba50897;
                            :organisationDescription "Brum Radio is a volunteer-led, independent station showcasing the best of Birmingham’s music, arts and culture. We’re passionate about giving space to local voices and creative projects that don’t always find a home on mainstream radio. Our volunteers are at the heart of everything we do, helping us keep the station vibrant, diverse and community-driven.";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/44ff7328-c65a-48a4-8ff5-210deec21dab_2025-08-26T21:12:35.012Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Brum Radio";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://brumradio.com/>.
id:68ae266f1c518088cda64ad5 :activityDescription """Brum Radio is Birmingham’s independent arts and music station, run entirely by volunteers. We’re looking for an Assistant Head of Production to help keep our live shows, podcasts, and recordings running smoothly.

In this role, you’ll support the Head of Production in coordinating studio activity, making sure programmes are well-prepared, recorded, and delivered to schedule. You’ll be involved in tasks such as:

Scheduling and supporting volunteer producers and presenters

Coordinating recording sessions and podcast production

Liaising with presenters about assets and deadlines

Helping to troubleshoot production issues as they arise

Contributing to ideas on how to improve our workflows and keep the station running efficiently

We’re looking for someone with an interest in radio, media, or creative production, and the skills to keep projects organised and on track. You’ll need to be clear in your communication, confident working independently, and happy collaborating with a diverse creative team.

This is a flexible volunteer role — there are no set office hours, but some availability during the week is important to keep on top of scheduling and communication. You don’t need to be available 24/7; what matters is being dependable, adaptable, and proactive in keeping things moving.

What you’ll gain

Experience in a lively independent station at the heart of Birmingham’s creative community

Opportunities to develop production, coordination, and leadership skills

Being part of a welcoming, volunteer-led team passionate about radio, music, and culture""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68ae234e5b749350cdd0ec72;
                            :activityTitle "Ongoing Volunteer Role: Assistant Head of Production";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68ae26f9d6ce88e6388cf744 :address "54-57 Allison Street, Birmingham";
                            :latitude "52.4784133"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.8896217"^^xsd:double.
id:68ae74f7a754285e1b266bca :activityDescription """**Reserve:**

South Stack is an ideal reserve to watch seabirds and choughs, with high cliffs, caves and offshore stacks, backed by 316ha/780 acres of heathland (Holyhead Mountain and Penrhosfeilw Common), and 43ha of coastal grass pasture. The RSPB Visitor Hub is located at the heart of the site and provides retail and catering facilities for around 185,000 visitors annually. Ellin's Tower observatory, is situated on the cliff edge overlooking the sea cliffs and auk colonies.

**Why We Want You:**

In our Visitor Hub, you will be welcoming customers and ensuring they make the most from their visit by letting them know what they can see on the reserve and directing them to points of interest. It’s a great way to engage with visitors and share your passion for nature and the RSPB.

There is a viewing area at Ellin's Tower, with binoculars and telescopes. You will be talking to visitors about wildlife on the reserve, walking routes and providing further assistance as required. 
At busy times, you will also be directing cars to park sensibly.

Please note volunteer placements are for a minimum of one week and the usual arrival/departure day is Friday. Your first day volunteering will be on Saturday and your last day will be on Thursday.

**What’s In It For You:**

South Stack is an ideal reserve to watch seabirds, with high cliffs, caves and offshore stacks, backed by the maritime heathland of Holyhead Mountain. The duties are ideally suited to people with a love of birds who enjoy sharing their enthusiasm with others.

This is an excellent chance to make a genuine contribution to wildlife conservation. You will gain a valuable insight into the workings of Europe's largest conservation charity.

Accommodation is provided in Plas Nico, a small farmhouse, a 500m walk from the Visitor Hub & Ellin's Tower.

**Please do not tick 'willing to share' unless you are applying as a couple.**


**Requirements:**

Minimum age 18. Enthusiasm, ability to work as part of a team, interest in conservation and wildlife. Specialist knowledge is not required, but enthusiasm for birds is. Excellent people skills; friendly, relaxed and able to engage with visitors essential.

**Notes**

There is no charge to take part in the scheme.

It is your responsibility to cover the cost of your transport to and from the reserve, and to provide and cover the cost of your food during your stay.

The RSPB will provide accommodation and bed linen, together with basic services, free of charge for the period you are volunteering, and cover any expenses incurred as a necessary part of your work on the reserve.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Residential on Reserve (Less than 4 weeks) (South Stack Visitor Engagement)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68ae74f7a754285e1b266bcb :address "South Stack Cliffs, South Stack Road, Holyhead, Anglesey, LL65 1YH, Wales";
                            :latitude "53.305073"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-4.691016"^^xsd:double.
id:68ae74f8a754285e1b266bef :activityDescription """We are looking for work party volunteers to join us on our reserve at RSPB Fairburn Ings Nature Reserve on Wednesdays. RSPB nature reserves are special places for people and wildlife and we rely on volunteers to keeps these habitats thriving and in good shape. 

It might seem like these wild places have always just been there. But quite often we have to work really hard to maintain habitats to be in the best condition for wildlife. We have lost a lot of wild landscapes, so the small places that we do have, need a bit of extra care.  

As a Reserve Work Party Volunteer you will be joining a regular practical work party team to undertake practical habitat management tasks: 
- Cutting and clearing vegetation.
- Maintaining and managing tools and equipment. 
- Creating and maintaining visitor infrastructure, such as paths and benches. 
- Following annual work plans. 
- Following Health and Safety protocols. 

If you enjoy being outdoors, meeting new people and hands on conservation work, then this role could be the perfect opportunity for you. This role does involve a reasonable amount of physical activity, so will enable you to keep your fitness levels high, as well as helping your mental health and wellbeing. You will have the satisfaction of knowing that you are making a genuine contribution to nature conservation, and you might find it rewarding to safeguard nature in the area you are working to protect. 

We are a friendly and supportive team. If you are volunteering to learn new skills and gain knowledge about wildlife conservation work, you will find that our team will be more than happy to help you on that journey.

This work parties takes place on Wednesdays.

Unfortunately, this role is not suitable for under 18’s.

Thank you for reading this description of the role. Clicking on ‘more about this role’ will take you to the generic ‘role profile’ for this opportunity. A role profile is the family to which an opportunity belongs and informs the vetting and training requirements. We particularly recommend taking note of the disclaimer.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Practical Reserve Work (Reserve Assistant Fairburn Ings)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68ae74f8a754285e1b266bf0 :address "Fairburn Ings, Newton Lane, Castleford, Yorkshire West Riding, WF10 2BH, England";
                            :latitude "53.745685"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.324136"^^xsd:double.
id:68aebc90330528cfb780308c :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """NewBeginnings Charitable Trust (NCT)
Empowering Generations. Restoring Dignity. Building Futures.
NewBeginnings Charitable Trust (NCT) is a registered, grassroots-level non-profit organization headquartered in Andhra Pradesh, India. Operating in some of the most drought-prone and underserved districts of the state, NCT is dedicated to uplifting the lives of women and children—especially girls—trapped in cycles of generational poverty, violence, and systemic neglect.
Guided by the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly SDG 1 (No Poverty), SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being), SDG 4 (Quality Education), SDG 5 (Gender Equality), and SDG 13 (Climate Action), NCT adopts a multi-pronged, community-based approach to drive lasting change
Our Key Interventions:
1. Educational/Sponsorship support for children from vulnerable communities.
2. Livelihood support & Skills Training for women and youth from marginalised communities (also for victims of sexual and gender-based violence towards economic empowerment)
3. Education centers/Age-Appropriate education for never-to-school / early school dropout children.
4. Formation of Micro-Credit society/groups at the rural villages/indigenous communities.
5. Educational and sensitisation awareness/seminars/workshops
 - Prevention of child marriages, human trafficking, child labour and school dropouts.
- Protecting women and girls from sexual and gender-based violence
- Drugs and psychotropic substance abuse: Educating and sensitizing
- Preventing young people aged 6-28 years from becoming addicted to drugs and psychotropic substances
6. Rejuvenating agriculture lands and natural farming to overcome droughts and migration.""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/50dc7b2b-b9d3-4f98-8c9d-85e7ed43e8e8_2025-10-06T05:08:10.278Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "NewBeginnings Charitable Trust";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.nctindia.org/>.
id:68aec3af66edfddaf20c2a71 :activityDescription """&A Carer Groups
Carers often have challenging experiences with people’s attitudes towards their caring role and can feel that they cannot talk to family members, friends, or other organisations.   The D&A groups offer carers a safe space to talk through their concerns and really be heard.  Carers can talk in confidence about any issues and know they will not be judged, having an understanding from others in the groups, and feel like they are not alone.

The role of the volunteer:
The D&A carer group volunteer will support the group alongside another volunteer or a CSWS staff member, creating a non-judgemental safe environment, for carers to talk about their caring roles and not feel stigmatized. 

The D&A volunteer will:
•	Welcome and support new and regular attendees to the groups.
•	Assist in preparing and closing the meeting space. (This may include moving chairs and tables, depending on the venue).
•	Support with tea and coffee.
•	listen and respond to issues and concerns raised by carers at the group, where possible, with information and signposting to local and national resources.  Arrange support with a CSWS member of staff for more complex issues and concerns. 
•	Have pre- and post-group ‘briefings’ with the other volunteer and CSWS staff member. If you have any concerns, you will be able to discuss with the D&A specialist or a member of the community team.
•	Support the D&A specialist with planning the group where needed.  
•	Complete volunteer records and upload receipts for any expenses.
•	Attend regular check-ins with a Volunteer Mentor.

""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68930c2c566fafd42b4b55f7;
                            :activityTitle "Face-to-Face Drug and Alcohol Carer (D&A) Group Volunteers CSWS ";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68aec3af66edfddaf20c2a72 :address "34 Mill Rd, Worthing BN11 5DR, UK";
                            :latitude "50.8143829"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-0.396193"^^xsd:double.
