                            :longitude "0.8392470999999999"^^xsd:double.
id:68b9d069c0c943b0a75c187f :activityDescription """DalesBus is urgently looking for new volunteers to help us to provide, promote and develop DalesBus services into and around the Yorkshire Dales.

Weekend DalesBus services into and around the Yorkshire Dales.are managed by Dales and Bowland Community Interest Company, a volunteer-run company owned by charity Friends of the Dales.

The public are showing that they need our services more than ever and we urgently need more help to rise to the challenge of maintaining and developing our services.

We are particularly keen to find people with interest/knowledge/skills/ideas in:
Recruiting, managing and supporting volunteers 
Marketing bus and train services - including tasks such as producing online and printed materials, managing social media, leaflet distribution and keeping bus stop timetables updated
Engaging with local communities, raising awareness of DalesBus
Generating new ideas to increase DalesBus’ positive impact
Leadership, strategy and governance of organisations

If you share our passion for improving lives within and around the Yorkshire Dales by bus and think you may be able to help, we’d love to hear from you. We will offer support to help you settle into any specific roles you take on. We would really appreciate offers of help at all levels.

The Board of Directors meets around four times a year at mutually convenient times and locations, in person or online, with directors and other volunteers carrying out various tasks throughout the year, depending on the responsibilities they agree to take on.

We actively welcome expressions of interest from people from a diverse range of backgrounds. Everyone volunteering with us should be willing to commit to the values set out on our website.

Please email kevin@dalesandbowland.com as soon as possible before 22nd September to express interest, providing information about the interest/knowledge/skills/ideas and time you might be able to share. Feel free to ask any questions, and please don’t hesitate to promote this opportunity to anyone else you know who might be interested.

We will respond to questions and expressions of interest as quickly as we can on a rolling basis, and look forward to hearing from you shortly.
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:6425f9b2f2d4b77a6e15f3d9;
                            :activityTitle "Volunteer";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68b9d069c0c943b0a75c1880 :address "From Home";
                            :latitude "52.5647271"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.4671882"^^xsd:double.
id:68ba5291e811b24fc5e86f90 :activityDescription """Do you enjoy meeting people and shops with lovely things?  
 
Our Darts Farm retail volunteers love making visitors welcome in our shop and helping them choose from the delightful array of RSPB products.  
 
What’s involved?  The role is varied so there will always be something you enjoy...

- Providing a warm welcome to our visitors. 
- Help with enquiries about our products. 
- Progressing sales through the till. 
- Helping with stock taking and refilling the shelves. 
- Keeping the shop looking clean and inviting. 
 
We would ideally like your help once a week, but we are flexible and you can choose a half day slot on our rota to fit in around your other commitments.
 
This role will particularly suit you if you...

- Love meeting people and are keen to give each customer the best experience.
- Enjoy learning about our stock and are enthusiastic in telling customers about it (this doesn’t have to include binoculars and telescopes unless you want to specialise in this!). 
- Are confident in handling money and sales transactions (training will be given). 
- Want to support the RSPB by generating extra income.  
  
It’s a great chance to...

- Meet a wide range of people and help them enjoy their visit. Talking with people is brilliant for our mental health too.
- Make new friends as you become part of the retail team. We are a lovely bunch!   
- Develop your customer service skills and experience. 
- Know that you’re doing something to help support the work of saving wildlife. 
 
You’ll also be volunteering in a lovely light and airy space looking out over Bowling Green Marsh and our birdfeeders are constantly busy with birds. There is always something to see outside our enormous shop window! 
 
There is plenty of free parking and the number 57 bus (between Exeter & Exmouth) stops right outside Darts Farm. We can cover the cost of you getting here too. 
  
A full induction to the role will be given, as well as training in operating the till and there is always someone around to give help or guidance if you need. We’re a very supportive team and you will be made to feel very welcome.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Retail Volunteer (RSPB Darts Farm)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68ba5291e811b24fc5e86f91 :address "Darts Farm, Topsham Road, Exeter, Devon, EX3 0QH, England";
                            :latitude "50.684714"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-3.44828"^^xsd:double.
id:68ba8b3ad8abe3a654cc8bd1 :activityDescription """The opportunity is to shadow BAAT staff in organising and delivering this year’s annual conference.

The opportunity will include:

- two half days in the office or online with our team to learn about organising an in-person event
- supporting our team for a full day at our annual conference, shadowing and supporting the Events Officer and Senior Communications Officer, and observing how a professional in-person event is run.
- an opportunity to review the day with the Events Officer in an online call, share feedback and ask questions

We will seek to make any reasonable adjustments required to suit individual needs, for example around travel times, accessibility support at the conference venue, or adjustments to the format of written materials shared during shadowing. Please contact us to discuss.

As part of our commitment to being a disability confident employer, this opportunity is open to disabled people who have faced barriers to work experience and employment due to disability. We understand this concept in relation to the social model of disability, which recognises that people are disabled by barriers in society, not by an impairment or difference.

You may not explicitly identify yourself as ‘disabled’ but nonetheless be eligible for this work shadowing. Please see the government’s definition of disability for guidance.

This opportunity might be suitable for those currently out of work, that are studying or recently graduated, or someone looking to change career and gain a greater understanding of a role in events.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:66d1d9761bebf49752aa30cf;
                            :activityTitle "Work shadowing opportunity at BAAT annual conference";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68ba8b3ad8abe3a654cc8bd2 :address "183 Euston Rd., Camden Rd, London NW1 2BE, UK";
                            :latitude "51.5259046"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-0.1338783"^^xsd:double.
id:68ba9c25ad25bf0b6094dd6b :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription "Ivison Trust (formerly Pace) is a national charity working to keep children safe from exploitation by supporting their parents, disrupting the exploiters and working in partnership with police and family services. We also train professionals to support affected children and their parents using a trauma-informed, family-centric approach.";
                            :organisationName "Ivison Trust";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://ivisontrust.org.uk/>.
id:68ba9d3c63504d598f56968a :activityDescription """This unique role will give you the opportunity to use your compassionate listening skills to make a difference to the lives of parents and carers whose children are being criminally or sexually exploited. This is a remote volunteer role that you can do from your home, where you will talk to affected parents and carers over the phone.

Be the Voice That Makes a Difference
Every day, parents and carers reach out to Ivison Trust because their children are being exploited. They’re scared, overwhelmed, and often feel completely alone. That’s where you come in.
Last year, our amazing call line volunteers supported over 550 parents and carers — offering them a safe space to be heard, understood, and supported. Just by picking up the phone, you could be that calm voice in a storm, helping someone feel less isolated and more hopeful.

“Talking to someone who truly listened helped me feel less alone. It gave me the strength to keep going.”

Why Volunteer with Us?
Make a Powerful Impact
Be there for someone when they need it most. Every call you take helps break the silence, reduce isolation, and empower families facing exploitation.
Full Training and Ongoing Support
We’ll give you everything you need to feel confident. From comprehensive training to hands-on guidance, our Volunteer Coordinator and Ivison Trust managers will support you every step of the way — before, during, and after each call.
Build Incredible Skills
This is more than volunteering — it’s personal growth. You’ll develop valuable skills in empathy, active listening, and managing challenging conversations.
Ready to Be Part of Something Meaningful?
Volunteering on our call line is your chance to do good, grow personally, and become part of a compassionate community working to make real change. Whether you’re looking to give back, gain experience, or learn new skills, this is your opportunity to be part of something truly impactful.

Location: Remote, from your home
Commitment required: Ideally one 2 or 3 hour evening shift per month on either a Friday or Saturday evening.

Our Support Line Operator Volunteers will be supported during their session by a shift leader and an on-call manager. Volunteers will receive regular support, supervision, and opportunities to connect with peers.

Equality and Diversity
We are committed to equal opportunities and anti-discrimination practices. We are striving to represent the diverse communities that we support and welcome applications from underrepresented groups.

What we offer
* An accredited training programme
* Supervision from compassionate, experienced parent support workers
* Opportunities for volunteer peer support and collaborative learning""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68ba9c25ad25bf0b6094dd6b;
                            :activityTitle "Support Line Volunteer ";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68ba9da663504d598f569718 :address "From Home";
                            :latitude "52.5647271"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.4671882"^^xsd:double.
id:68baaf2bf2d20a652f4e024a :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089d,
                                   id:64093b552eed5dcadba508a3;
                            :organisationDescription "";
                            :organisationName "the Children's Country House at Sudbury";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/peak-district-derbyshire/the-childrens-country-house-at-sudbury>.
id:68bab1a108b8f20323ba06a9 :activityDescription """The Children’s Country House at Sudbury is a playful, immersive heritage experience that has 
been designed by, with and for children. A place where children and families are able to laugh, 
wonder, learn and engage with the buildings, gardens, collections and stories at Sudbury.
If only these walls could talk! That’s why our House Experience volunteers make such a difference. 
They give everyone a warm welcome, bring stories from Sudbury to life and help people get the 
best our of their day. 

By getting involved, you could…
• Bring the Children’s Country House and Gardens to life for Children and their families.
• Inspire fun and creativity in children through an engaging and warm approach.
• Volunteer at a beautiful and inspiring location, that is packed with history
• Get to know Sudbury and our visitors
• Be part of a varied and enthusiastic team and form new friendships
• Enjoy new experiences and learn something new everyday
• Develop and share your knowledge
• Know you have made a difference to someone’s day out
You’d also be joining Europe’s largest conservation charity and the wider National Trust team 
proudly looking after 780 miles of coastline, over 250,000 hectares of land, over 500 historic 
houses, castles, ancient monuments, gardens, parks and nature reserves (including lighthouses, 
villages, pubs and a gold mine!). We are a unique charity welcoming millions of people each year 
and with a vision to open up places for the benefit of everyone, for ever. 

What’s involved?
• All our volunteer roles have three shared characteristics, no matter what part of the property 
you are supporting. We expect our whole team to provide exceptional service, uphold the very 
best presentation standards and engage with visitors to build a connection between them and 
Sudbury, which will bring them back time and time again. 
• Creating a warm welcome for everyone who visits
• Sharing inspirational stories, curious facts and practical information or simply the space for 
visitors to wander at their own pace
• Responding to the different needs of different visitors, including people with additional access 
needs
• Finding out what visitors are looking for from their visit and helping them decide how to get the 
best out of their day
• Learning more about Sudbury so you can share all that’s special 
• To support the Collections & House Team protect our historic objects and engage visitors in 
our preventive conservation work 
• You are important in making sure things run smoothly and safely as possible – this includes 
getting to know health and safety, safeguarding and evacuation procedures.
• Being part of a team that may need to adapt and change in response to local needs, National 
Trust priorities or changes to government guidelines e.g .Covid

We want all our volunteers to feel welcome and to have an equally positive experience when 
volunteering with us. So, volunteers will: 

• Be provided with the necessary induction, training, equipment and reasonable adjustments to 
do this role well and safely
• Be offered expenses to cover agreed travel costs between home and place of volunteering.
• Have remote access to support, news and rotas via our volunteer website
This role will suit you if you are …
• Full of enthusiasm and enjoy engaging and inspiring visitors of all ages
• Someone who loves being around people – you’ll meet a lot each day!
• Interested in Sudbury – you don’t need to know all of the history, but as a House Experience 
Volunteer you’ll enjoy picking up lots of facts and figures
Being enthusiastic about engaging with children and their families is far more important than any 
particular skills or knowledge for this role (we can help you find out all you need to know).
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68baaf2bf2d20a652f4e024a;
                            :activityTitle "House Experience Volunteer";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68bab1a108b8f20323ba06aa :address "Sudbury Hall, Sudbury, Ashbourne";
                            :latitude "52.886224"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.7656803"^^xsd:double.
id:68bab262501bc571243511a2 :activityDescription """The Children’s Country House at Sudbury is a playful, immersive heritage experience that has 
been designed by, with and for children. A place where children and families are able to laugh, 
wonder, learn and engage with the buildings, gardens, collections and stories at Sudbury.
Our Visitor Welcome volunteers create a positive first impression with a warm welcome; helping 
visitors get all they need from their visit. You’ll also help us provide a personalised thank you, 
leaving people with a positive lasting impression.

By getting involved, you could…
• Bring the Children’s Country House and Gardens to life for Children and their families.
• Inspire fun and creativity in children through an engaging and warm approach.
• Join an enthusiastic and varied team and form new friendships
• Volunteer at a beautiful and inspiring location
• Let your personality shine, and build connections with our visitors and wider property team
• Know you have made a difference to someone’s day out

You’d also be joining Europe’s largest conservation charity and the wider National Trust team 
proudly looking after 780 miles of coastline, over 250,000 hectares of land, over 500 historic houses, 
castles, ancient monuments, gardens, parks and nature reserves (including lighthouses, villages, 
pubs and a gold mine!). We are a unique charity welcoming millions of people each year and with a 
vision to open up places for the benefit of everyone, for ever. 

What’s involved?
• All our volunteer roles have three shared characteristics, no matter what part of the property 
you are supporting. We expect our whole team to provide exceptional service, uphold the very 
best presentation standards and engage with visitors to build a connection between them and 
Sudbury, which will bring them back time and time again 
• Creating warm welcomes for every visitor, putting their needs first
• Sharing information, answering questions and helping visitors get the most out of their visit
• Giving personalised goodbyes and heartfelt thank yous
• Multi-tasking across the site to help us keep the property looking fabulous, reporting where 
something may need attention
• Looking out for ways to do the little things that make a big difference
• Helping visitors get their bearings and informing on the best routes around the property 
depending on their needs
• Helping the team in achieving our ‘growing support’ targets, by promoting membership, 
fundraising and Gift Aid
• Finding out what’s on offer at Sudbury and other local sites, so you can promote the National 
Trust and deepen their connection to our cause
• Keeping in touch with the wider team, so you know what’s happening
• You are important in making sure things run smoothly and safely as possible – this includes 
getting to know health and safety, safeguarding and evacuation procedures
• Being part of a team that may need to adapt and change in response to local needs, National 
Trust priorities or changes to government guidelines e.g .Covid

We want all our volunteers to feel welcome and to have an equally positive experience when 
volunteering with us. So, volunteers will: 
• Be provided with the necessary induction, training, equipment and reasonable adjustments to do 
this role well and safely
• Be offered expenses to cover agreed travel costs between home and place of volunteering
• Have remote access to support, news and rotas via our volunteer website

This role will suit you if you are …
• Someone who loves meeting people and being part of a team
• Looking for new experiences
• Wanting to utilise your communication skills or develop them further
• Someone who enjoys a busy environment
• Cheerful – even when a coach party has just arrived and it’s a little bit hectic!
Being enthusiastic about engaging with children and their families is far more important than any 
particular skills or knowledge for this role (we can help you find out all you need to know).""";
