                            :organisationDescription "The National Football Museum is an independent charity dedicated to safeguarding and promoting football’s heritage for everyone. Through its museum in Manchester and research centre at Preston North End’s Deepdale stadium, the organisation works to collect, conserve, and share the stories that have shaped the game. It collaborates with communities, schools, and researchers to inspire learning, spark pride, and ensure football’s cultural significance is celebrated for generations to come.";
                            :organisationName "The Research Centre at The National Football Museum";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://nationalfootballmuseum.com/>.
id:68c28fbc9c21fdf2e9d1bb12 :activityDescription """Love football? Fascinated by history and great stories?

The Research Centre at Deepdale Stadium – part of the National Football Museum – is looking for new volunteers to help us share and celebrate football’s incredible heritage. Whether you’re a lifelong fan or simply curious about culture, this is your chance to get involved.

Tour Guide Volunteer - Bring Deepdale’s history to life with stories and passion. Tours run around once a month on a rota, so you’ll join a team of guides who make each visit memorable. A great role if you enjoy talking to people and sharing heritage.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68c28e649c21fdf2e9d1ba3c;
                            :activityTitle "Tour Guide Volunteer";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68c28fbc9c21fdf2e9d1bb13 :address "Sir Tom Finney Way, Preston";
                            :latitude "53.7743934"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.6897673"^^xsd:double.
id:68c2913cfcd524ffb538b01c :activityDescription """Love football? Fascinated by history and great stories?

The Research Centre at Deepdale Stadium – part of the National Football Museum – is looking for new volunteers to help us share and celebrate football’s incredible heritage. Whether you’re a lifelong fan or simply curious about culture, this is your chance to get involved.

Collections Volunteer - Get hands-on with football’s past by researching, cataloguing and caring for the collection. Roles are project-based, usually one day a week for a set time, giving you the chance to focus on completing specific tasks and contributing to the collection’s future.

Key Dates

Here’s what happens next:

1. Experience our work – We’d love for you to come along and see our Match Day Exhibit this October. This will give you a feel for the kind of activities and stories we share.

2. Volunteering Insight Day – Join us on Wednesday 20th November at Deepdale Stadium for an introduction to volunteering with us. You’ll meet the team, learn more about the roles available, and hear from current volunteers about their experiences.

3. Volunteer Training – For those who wish to continue, we’ll deliver a training session on Wednesday 29th January. This will prepare you for starting your volunteer role with us in the new year.

Project-Based Collections Work
Volunteers may be assigned specific projects focused on a particular group of objects or 
collection area. This can include:
 - Auditing the items to verify their presence, condition, and documentation 
 - Creating or updating inventories and ensuring records are accurate in the system 
 - Organising, labelling, and rehousing items as needed  
 - Cleaning and tidying storage areas to improve accessibility and care 
 - Identifying any gaps, duplicates, or issues in the collection data 
This end-to-end approach gives volunteers the opportunity to take ownership of a project, 
contribute meaningfully to collections care, and gain hands-on experience in museum 
practice.

Time commitment 
Collections volunteers are usually assigned a specific project focused on a group of objects 
or a collection area. This may involve auditing, inventory creation, organising, and 
improving storage conditions. Volunteers typically commit to one day per week for the 
duration of the project, with scheduling arranged in advance to suit availability.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68c28e649c21fdf2e9d1ba3c;
                            :activityTitle "Collections Volunteer";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68c2913cfcd524ffb538b01d :address "Sir Tom Finney Way, Preston";
                            :latitude "53.7743934"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.6897673"^^xsd:double.
id:68c2925876d0058918bbea97 :activityDescription """To support the day-to-day facilitation of researcher visits to the collections held at the 
Research Centre located at Preston North End Deepdale Stadium.
 Main areas of responsibility 
- Welcoming and assisting researchers during their visits  
- Locating and preparing relevant items from the collection in advance 
- Ensuring safe handling and return of objects after use 
- Supporting staff with documentation and tracking of research enquiries 
- Working within the Volunteer Agreement guidelines and related organisational 
policies (Safeguarding, Health and Safety, and Security) 
Time commitment 
This role requires a commitment of one day per week on Tuesdays, for a six-month 
period. After this time, we will recruit new volunteers to ensure a range of people have the 
opportunity to contribute and gain experience. 
Skills/characteristics required
 - Curious and research-minded – interested in supporting historical research and 
engaging with archival materials  
- Friendly and approachable – able to welcome researchers and provide helpful guidance 
during visits 
- Organised and attentive – comfortable managing schedules, preparing materials, and 
maintaining accurate records 
- Responsible and respectful – understands the importance of handling heritage items 
with care 
- Clear communicator – able to liaise confidently with staff and researchers, and ask 
questions when needed 
- Digitally literate – confident using computers for cataloguing, data entry, and managing 
visit documentation (training provided)

Key Dates

Here’s what happens next:

1. Experience our work – We’d love for you to come along and see our Match Day Exhibit this October. This will give you a feel for the kind of activities and stories we share.

2. Volunteering Insight Day – Join us on Wednesday 20th November at Deepdale Stadium for an introduction to volunteering with us. You’ll meet the team, learn more about the roles available, and hear from current volunteers about their experiences.

3. Volunteer Training – For those who wish to continue, we’ll deliver a training session on Wednesday 29th January. This will prepare you for starting your volunteer role with us in the new year.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68c28e649c21fdf2e9d1ba3c;
                            :activityTitle "Researcher Visits Volunteer";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68c2925876d0058918bbea98 :address "Sir Tom Finney Way, Preston";
                            :latitude "53.7743934"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-2.6897673"^^xsd:double.
id:68c2a09039250c80afe2eec2 :cause id:64093b552eed5dcadba5089f;
                            :organisationDescription """For 22 years, Raising Futures Kenya (England & Wales charity no.1181670) has worked alongside local people, and our partner NGO, in Kenya to develop projects which are owned by the community and create long-term, sustainable change to reduce poverty and improve livelihoods. 

Through our Seed of Hope training and support programme we enable 14-25 year olds (75% girls) to secure employment or start a small business, generating enough income to live above the poverty line. We provide free technical vocational and business skills training, alongside mental health and wellbeing support which has led to over 90% of graduates being in employment, self-employment or further education. Seed of Hope offers young people opportunity, dignity and hope. 
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/7ef8c65c-33d4-460c-995e-bd48fb0863ab_2025-09-11T10:12:30.968Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Raising Futures Kenya";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://www.raisingfutureskenya.org.uk/>.
id:68c2a22b4c326bf217e79092 :activityDescription """Role Summary
Responsibilities of all Trustees
Ensuring the organisation pursues its charitable objects (purposes), as defined in its governing document. 
Ensuring strategic objectives are developed and met in order to provide greater public benefit, namely to the young people and communities we work alongside in Kenya.
Ensuring that the organisation complies with its governing document (i.e. constitution or memorandum and articles of association), charity law and any other relevant legislation or regulations.
Ensuring that the organisation applies its resources exclusively in pursuit of its charitable objects (i.e. the charity must not spend money on activities that are not included in its own objects, however worthwhile or charitable those activities are) for the benefit of the public.
Ensuring that the organisation defines its goals and evaluates performance against agreed targets.
Safeguarding the good name and values of the organisation.
Ensuring the effective and efficient administration of the organisation, including having appropriate policies and procedures in place.
Ensuring the financial stability of the organisation.
Protecting and managing the property of the charity and ensuring the proper investment of the charity’s funds.
Following proper and formal arrangements for the appointment, supervision, support, appraisal and remuneration of the CEO.
In addition to the above statutory duties, each trustee should use any specific skills, knowledge or experience they have to help the board of trustees reach sound decisions. This may involve scrutinising board papers, leading discussions, focusing on key issues, providing advice and guidance on new initiatives, or other issues in which the trustee has special expertise. 
Networking and partnership development
All Trustees are expected to assist Raising Futures Kenya to secure a sustainable and diverse funding base for the organisation, from seeking funding opportunities to galvanising personal and professional networks to generate income.
Act as a proud ambassador for Raising Futures Kenya, representing the charity at meetings and facilitating networking and funding opportunities as these arise. 
Join relevant networks and associations to further your understanding of the sector we are in, and to promote the work and best practices of the charity. 
Advise on fundraising 
Support the strategic direction of how funds are raised by the UK and Kenya, in terms of shifting the power.
Provide direct feedback to fundraising staff on approaches, bids or key organisations.
Link Raising Futures Kenya with key contacts or organisations in the fundraising sector.
Person Specification
The ideal candidate will have the following skills and experience;  
Essential:
Experience as a fundraiser in an international development organisation.
Understanding of the localisation or shift the power agenda.
A commitment to the organisation, and willingness to devote approximately 6 hours a month and attend Board meetings.
Understanding of the regulatory responsibilities of a Charity Incorporated Organisation (or willingness to learn) 
A willingness to speak your mind with respect, tact and diplomacy. 
Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. 
Honesty and integrity. We’re a values led organisation and want to ensure that everyone who joins us shares our values of honesty and integrity.
Teamwork and commitment. We’re a small organisation and rely on each other to lean in and play a part in achieving our objectives. We believe in getting the work done, but having fun along the way so our meetings are often light-hearted.
Passionate about furthering the impact of our work, in whatever way you can. For example utilising your networks to share the work we do or to seek donations.

Desirable: 
Competent in understanding finances.
Knowledge and experience of programmes in;
Education, Technical Vocational Training or Entrepreneurship
Mental Health and Wellbeing
Young people
Girls and women
Income generation
Farming/agriculture
Programmes in Kenya
We are always looking for candidates who have lived experience of any of the following;
Kenya
East Africa
Technical Vocational Training

We are committed to a culture of inclusivity.  We are able to grow and learn better if we have a diverse organisation formed of the collective sum of individual differences, lived experiences, knowledge and self-expression.  We don’t want to exist in an echo chamber.  In recruiting for our team we welcome the unique perspectives you can bring in terms of culture, education, opinions, ethnicity, race, sex, gender identity, values, age, religion, disability, sexual orientation, beliefs, nation of origin and languages spoken.  
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68c2a09039250c80afe2eec2;
                            :activityTitle "Trustee with Fundraising Experience (remote opportunity)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
id:68c2a22b4c326bf217e79093 :address "From Home";
