id:68cbe1e6020c8feb31558c2a :cause id:64093b532eed5dcadba50895;
                            :organisationDescription """Abbeyfield has always been more than housing. It is a movement built on community, companionship, and the belief that no older person should feel alone. Nearly 70 years on from Richard Carr-Gomm’s first Abbeyfield house, that ethos continues to inspire societies, volunteers, trustees, and staff across the Abbeyfield Family.

Abbeyfield England (AE) is the membership body for all Abbeyfield societies in England. Abbeyfield England’s role is to support and connect societies, strengthen them through practical services, and amplify their collective voice, so that each can thrive as part of the wider Abbeyfield Family.

Who We Are
•	A Membership Body – accountable directly to societies, governed in their interests, and shaped by their voice.
•	Member-Led – our Board includes representatives from societies, ensuring accountability to members, alongside independent trustees who bring external expertise.
•	Grounded in the Abbeyfield Ethos – we uphold the values of companionship, community, and care that have defined Abbeyfield since its beginning, staying true to the founding belief that no older person should be lonely.
•	Part of the Abbeyfield Family – while Abbeyfield is an international movement, our role is to serve societies across England, Scotland, Cymru, and Northern Ireland within the UK context.
Our Role
Abbeyfield England exists to:
•	Support societies by equipping trustees and staff with the tools and knowledge they need to manage effectively.
•	Connect members by bringing societies together to exchange ideas, tackle common challenges, and access shared solutions.
•	Strengthen societies with services and resources that reduce pressure and build confidence.
•	Amplify the collective voice of societies so they are heard by government, policymakers, and the public.
•	Celebrate Abbeyfield’s ethos and history and promote the difference societies make in their communities.
""";
                            :organisationImage <https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/doit2.0-production-uploads/OrganizationPicture/b8a92a5e-b39c-49ac-97ce-2c64c23b3f21_2025-09-18T10:41:41.960Z.jpeg>;
                            :organisationName "Abbeyfield England";
                            :organisationWebsite <https://abbeyfield.com/>.
id:68cbe6eb2010767c17d84a00 :activityDescription """ 
Volunteer: Chair of the Board – Abbeyfield England

Help thousands of older people live with dignity, community, and purpose.

Voluntary role (expenses paid) • Hybrid (national & online)

Do you believe every older person deserves to live in a connected, caring community? Abbeyfield England is seeking a new Chair to lead our Board through the next chapter of our work, supporting a unique national network of local Abbeyfield member societies.

Abbeyfield England is a national membership body. We are a registered charity.   Our member societies operate not for profit houses and homes across England, providing safe, supported, and sociable housing and care for older people – ranging from small volunteer-led houses to large extra-care services. Abbeyfield’s work has enjoyed longstanding Royal Patronage, currently held by His Majesty King Charles III. The movement’s roots go back to 1956. 

The Role
Abbeyfield England was established in 2022 as the new umbrella body for the movement. Now that we have the main building blocks in place, we’re seeking a new Chair to lead a collaborative, strategic and purposeful Board through the next period of development, including our 70th Anniversary year in 2026.

You’ll:
•	Provide strategic leadership and ensure strong, values-led governance
•	Support and appraise the General Secretary and ensure an effective Board culture
•	Build trust and engagement across our federated membership
•	Represent Abbeyfield England with pride, both within our movement and externally

The Chair will also play a key ambassadorial role as the Abbeyfield movement marks 70 years of Abbeyfield’s impact, including speaking opportunities, profile-raising, and engaging with member societies across the country.

Time Commitment
The anticipated time commitment is 15–20 days per year, including:
•	4 formal Board meetings (held in-person across our member societies)
•	Biennial Membership Conference
•	Quarterly Finance Committee & Operations Committee meetings (Chair may attend)
•	Twice-yearly HR Committee meetings
•	Occasional representation at national or movement-wide events

We will ensure a supportive induction and an optional co-chairing handover with the current Interim Chair from December 2025 to March 2026.

About You
We’re not looking for status. We’re looking for someone who believes in what Abbeyfield stands for and who can bring warmth, good judgement, and strategic thinking to this leadership role.

Ideally you would have experience within a membership organisation, and perhaps already have chaired a Board.  In addition, we are seeking
•	Leadership and governance experience in a charity, housing, care, or complex service organisation
•	Strong listening, communication, and relationship-building skills
•	Financial literacy and comfort with strategic risk
•	A passion for helping older people live connected and fulfilling lives

We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and will make reasonable adjustments throughout the process.

How to Apply
Please send:
•	Your CV (required)
•	You are also welcome to include a link to your LinkedIn profile
•	A short covering letter (or video) explaining why you’d like to Chair Abbeyfield England and what you would bring to the role

Send to: AE.Board@abbeyfieldengland.com
Deadline: Monday 21 October 2025
Interviews: Week commencing 3 or 10 November 2025
Board appointment: 2 December 2025
Co-chairing handover: December 2025 – March 2026
Chair formally in post: April 2026
If you’d like more information or an informal conversation with the Chair before applying, email AE.Board@abbeyfieldengland.com

Abbeyfield is a movement of people who believe in ageing well - in homes filled with warmth, community, and care. Join us to help make that vision a reality.

Registered Charity No. 1213760
""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:68cbe1e6020c8feb31558c2a;
                            :activityTitle "Chair - Abbeyfield England ";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68cbea54f68aa4db5bdda78b :address "https://www.abbeyfield.com/";
                            :latitude "52.5647271"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-1.4671882"^^xsd:double.
id:68cbf07231d9a5cd56e276c1 :activityDescription """Get into the festive spirit by volunteering at X Factor's G4’s Christmas Concert. Hear wonderful renditions of Christmas classics, even join in on a few tunes yourself, all while supporting the charity Missing People. This is a fantastic opportunity to volunteer at a truly enjoyable evening so don’t miss out.

Please register through the application form here - https://forms.volunteero.org/view/a7f14263-69dc-4ad8-9d40-f33f0bac4de0

Volunteers will be acting as ushers, showing guests their seats on arrival. You will also be helping to collect donations during intervals and at the end of the night, standing around the venue holding a bucket or tap to donate card machine.

For each venue, we also need a Lead Volunteer. This involves collecting and counting the cash donations at the end of the night and transferring to the charity the following day. You would receive specific instructions for this.

No training is needed for the role. You will receive a simple email briefing a few weeks in advance, and a full briefing from the team on your arrival on the night.

The event is from 19:30 – 21:30, but we’d ask for you to arrive at the venue from 18:00 and expect to finish at 22:00. Dates across November & December, around UK. """;
                            :activityOrganisation id:6425cdf8a0624f3ab9fb196a;
                            :activityTitle "Christmas Concert Ushers - Missing People";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation false.
