                            :longitude "-0.0274353"^^xsd:double.
id:68df3c61433a0589a000e24f :activityDescription """Help Save the Slavonian Grebe – Become a Data Volunteer

Slavonian Grebes are in trouble. Once thriving on over 30 lochs in Inverness-shire, their numbers have plummeted—from 80 pairs in the 1970s to just 20 pairs on 14 lochs in 2024. With support from the Nature Restoration Fund, we’re working to uncover the causes of this decline—and we need your help.

As a Data Volunteer, you’ll play a vital role in processing and analysing bird and habitat data collected during the 2025 breeding season. Tasks include digitising survey maps and forms, reviewing trail and underwater camera footage, and recording key observations. Your support will directly inform conservation efforts and help us understand the role of habitat, diet, and environmental pressures in the species’ decline.

This is a flexible, home-based role suitable for anyone in the UK with good attention to detail, IT skills, and a passion for nature. We ask for a minimum commitment of 15 hours between October and December 2025. You’ll receive full guidance and support from our friendly project team.

As a Data Volunteer you'll help with processing existing data, digitising handwritten maps and survey forms, and to review & analyse trail camera and underwater camera imagery. 

This role will include the following tasks and the team will provide full training and support: 
• Reviewing trail camera images, identifying and recording any birds or mammals or any human disturbance captured by the cameras. 
• Reviewing videos captured on underwater cameras and recording the type and number of fish appearing in the videos.  
• Digitising scanned copies of survey maps or forms by entering the data into spreadsheets or databases.  
• This role may be shared between several volunteers assigned to the role 

You will receive guidance and support from the project team, but we are ideally looking for people with:
• Good communication skills
• Attention to detail and the ability to focus on a task
• Ability to accurately transfer data from a handwritten format to a digital format.  
• IT skills, including creating and managing spreadsheets. 
• Good organisational skills

This role is primarily a home based role, it can be undertaken from anywhere in the UK and you can mostly carry out the role in your own time. You can carry out this role using your own laptop or computer, equipment can be provided if required. 

You will receive written instructions and guidance from project staff, they will be on hand to discuss the tasks with you via video call or phone call. Project staff will give guidance and training, we ask that volunteers are able to commit at least 15 hours of their time to this role between October and December 2025. 

Make a real impact for nature—join us in protecting one of Scotland’s most iconic birds.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "RSPB Team Member (Saving Slavonian Grebes – Remote Data Volunteer)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68df3c61433a0589a000e250 :address "Etive House, IV2 3BW, Etive House, Beechwood Park, Inverness, Highland, IV2 3BW, United Kingdom";
                            :latitude "57.472161"^^xsd:double;
                            :longitude "-4.187902"^^xsd:double.
id:68df3c62433a0589a000e27a :activityDescription """Duration six months - Starting in December 2025 or January 2026 and finishing in July 2026.

Why We Want You

We are looking for enthusiastic residential volunteers to join our friendly reserve team. You will be volunteering at the North East's flagship reserve helping key species such as breeding common tern, avocet, sand martin, bearded tit, bittern and many more.  As a residential volunteer, you will undertake all aspects of practical habitat management, survey and monitoring, estate management and species protection - depending on the time of the year, and will be able to develop essential skills and experience necessary for a successful career in wildlife conservation.


What’s In It For You

Residential Volunteers at Saltholme become fully fledged team members, working alongside staff and other passionate volunteers. You will gain first-hand experience of the daily operation of a wetland reserve through a diverse range of activities including tern island management, wet grassland and reedbed management and maintaining dragonfly ponds.  You will be given appropriate training and have the chance to develop the necessary skills required to start a career in conservation.  This will include habitat management techniques, experience in species monitoring, estate management and leading volunteer work parties. 

You will also gain first-hand knowledge of the workings of Europe's largest conservation charity whilst working at one of Northern England's most important nature reserves.

Nestled in the Tees Valley, surrounded by many industrial neighbours, Saltholme offers a dramatic landscape and stunning wildlife.  Hundreds of pairs of common terns breed on cockleshell islands in the summer and thousands of wildfowl spend the winter.  With dynamic habitats such as reedbeds, wet grassland, open pools and scrapes, Saltholme attracts a huge variety of wildlife.


Requirements

You must be at least 18 years old, with a full clean driving licence. You should be enthusiastic and have the ability to live and work as part of a close-knit team.  An interest in wildlife, the environment and its conservation is essential.  Tasks to be undertaken depend on the time of year and you should be prepared to undertake strenuous manual labour outside in all weathers.  A degree of flexibility is required, as weekend working on a rota will be necessary, with time off in lieu during the week.

Notes

This is a fantastic opportunity to be supported and develop your existing skills.  If you are looking for a career in environmental conservation this is a great place to start.  All appropriate training is given and free accommodation (including bills) is provided in the form of a spacious first floor flat with open views of the sea.  The flat is close to the reserve in the nearby seaside town of Seaton Carew with a less than ten-minute drive or short bus ride away.""";
                            :activityOrganisation id:64119bf12a79fbd15e8912b6;
                            :activityTitle "Residential on Reserve (More than 4 weeks) (RSPB Saltholme - Residential Conservation Volunteer)";
                            :allowsRemoteParticipation true.
id:68df3c62433a0589a000e27b :address "Saltholme, Seaton Carew Road, Stockton-On-Tees, Middlesbrough, Durham, TS2 1TU, England";
